.TH KOSH 1 "2026-10-25" "kosh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kosh \- a fast and debuggable POSIX- and Bash-compatible command shell,
formatter, and language server
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBkosh\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIscript\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]]
.br
\fBkosh \-c\fR \fIcommand\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
.br
\fBkosh \-s\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
.br
\fBkosh \-\-lint\fR [\fB\-c\fR \fIcommand\fR ...] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.br
\fBkosh \-\-format\fR [\fIfile\fR]
.br
\fBkosh \-\-lint \-\-format\fR [\fIfile\fR]
.br
\fBkosh\fR [\fB\-\-lint\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR] \fB\-\-apply\fR \fIfile\fR ...
.br
\fBkosh \-\-as\-language\-server\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kosh
is the Koshka shell, with a formatter, a language server, and script
diagnostics. It accepts POSIX shell syntax and the Bash extensions described
in this manual. The same grammar is available on Linux, macOS, and Windows.
.SH INVOCATION
The shell selects the input source in the following order. The
.B \-s
option selects standard input, one or more
.B \-c
options select their command strings, a script operand selects that file, and
.B \-i
or a terminal on standard input selects the interactive editor. Otherwise the
shell takes commands from standard input. When the options select several
sources, the shell
prints a warning and uses the first of them in that order, except that
.B \-s
with
.B \-i
starts the interactive editor when standard input is a terminal. The
interactive editor needs a terminal, so
.B \-i
without one fails after the startup files run.
.PP
The invocation basename selects an initial mood. The basename
.B sh
or
.B dash
selects the
.B sh
mood, and
.B bash
or
.B rbash
selects the
.B bash
mood.
.B rbash
also requests restricted mode. A dash at the start of argument zero, or
.BR \-l ,
selects login operation.
.PP
.B KOSH_FLAGS
supplies options before the command line, and a command-line option overrides
the same option from it.
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-format ,
.BR \-\-apply ,
.BR \-\-as\-language\-server ,
.BR \-\-rcfile ,
.BR \-\-init\-file ,
.BR \-\- ,
and every operand are ignored there. A secure execution ignores and removes
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
.PP
When argument zero begins with a dash and the options cannot be parsed, the
shell reports the error and enters rescue mode, which skips the startup and
settings files. When only KOSH_FLAGS fails to parse, the shell drops it and
keeps the command-line options and input source. When the command line fails
to parse, the shell keeps only
.B \-r
and takes commands from standard input, interactively on a terminal. Any other
invalid invocation, including an unknown value of
.BR \-\-mood ,
.BR \-\-tab\-selector ,
.BR \-\-init\-moods ,
.BR \-o ,
or
.BR \-O ,
exits with status 2.
.SH OPTIONS
An option given on the command line, on or off, overrides the settings files
and
.BR KOSHCONF .
.SS General options
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and notices, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-short\-version
Print the short version, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print help, then exit.
.SS POSIX options
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Run interactively.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stdin
Take commands from standard input. INVOCATION describes the order of input
sources.
.TP
.BR \-c " \fICOMMAND\fR, " \-\-command =\fICOMMAND\fR
Run COMMAND. The option may be repeated, and the strings run in order. A
string with a syntax error ends the shell with status 2, one that analysis
rejects ends it with status 1, and the later strings do not run.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-error\-exit
Exit after an unguarded failing command.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-no\-glob
Disable pathname expansion.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-one\-command
Read and run one command, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print each input line as it is read.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-xtrace
Print each command after expansion.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-export\-all
Mark each later assignment for export.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-no\-clobber
Keep a plain output redirection from overwriting an existing regular file.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-exec
Parse and analyze commands without running them. A compatibility mood reports
only the tiers that
.B \-W
selects.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-no\-unset
Treat the expansion of an unset variable as an error.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
Act as a login shell.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-m
The shell accepts these options and ignores them.
.TP
.BR \-o " \fINAME\fR, " +o " \fINAME\fR"
Enable or disable an option as
.B set \-o
and
.B set +o
do.
.B \-o posix
acts as
.BR \-\-posix .
An unknown option exits with status 2. In the
.B kosh
mood,
.B "+o nounset"
is ignored and
.B "+o pipefail"
exits with status 2.
.TP
.BR +a ", " +C ", " +e ", " +f ", " +n ", " +p ", " +t ", " +u ", " +v ", " +x
Turn the option of that letter off, as
.B set +e
does. +h and +m are accepted and ignored. The
.B set
letters \-B, \-E, \-H, \-P, \-b, and \-k are not command-line options and exit
with status 2.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option parsing. The next operand is the script.
.SS Bash options
.TP
.BR \-\-init\-file " \fIFILE\fR, " \-\-rcfile " \fIFILE\fR"
Use FILE as the interactive rc file in place of ~/.bashrc in an interactive
non-login Bash mood, and in place of the user rc script in an interactive
non-login
.B kosh
mood.
.TP
.B \-\-norc
Skip the interactive non-login Bash rc files, the user rc script, and a custom
rc file. A login shell, its profiles, and BASH_ENV are unaffected.
.TP
.BR \-O " \fINAME\fR, " +O " \fINAME\fR"
Turn the shopt option NAME on or off, as
.B shopt \-s
and
.B shopt \-u
do. An unknown option exits with status 2, and a read-only option such as
login_shell is ignored. In the
.B kosh
mood,
.B "+O failglob"
and
.B "\-O nullglob"
exit with status 2.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-restricted
Become restricted after the startup files finish.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-privileged
Enable privileged mode. ENV, BASH_ENV, and the rc script of an interactive
non-login
.B kosh
mood shell are not sourced, KOSHCONF is ignored and removed, and no settings
file is read. A secure execution, such as a setuid or
setgid invocation, always drops the elevated ids and skips every startup and
settings file. On Linux, such a shell whose real user is not root also clears
its capability sets, including the ambient set, and aborts if it can still
switch to root.
.TP
.B \-\-posix
Select the
.B bash-posix
mood, unless
.B \-\-mood
or
.B \-\-dumb
selects another.
.SS Compatibility options
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-mood =\fINAME\fR
Select the
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash ,
.BR sh ,
or
.B bash-posix
mood. Without this option, the first line of a script file operand selects the
mood. A bash shebang selects
.BR bash ,
an sh or dash shebang selects
.BR sh ,
and a kosh shebang selects
.BR kosh ,
directly or through
.BR env .
Any other interpreter, or no shebang, leaves the
.B kosh
mood.
.BR \-\-mood ,
.BR \-\-dumb ,
.BR \-\-posix ,
and an invocation basename such as sh or bash take precedence over the
shebang, and
.B \-\-mood
also takes precedence over
.B \-\-posix
and
.BR "\-o posix" .
The shebang is not used for
.BR \-c ,
standard input, or
.B \-\-lint
without
.BR \-I .
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-init\-moods =\fILIST\fR
Source the startup files of each mood in LIST, in order. Moods are separated
by commas, or the option is repeated.
Without this option, an interactive or login shell uses init_moods from the
settings, and any other shell uses the selected mood.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-enable\-mimicry
Run a script started as a command under the mood its shell shebang selects, by
the precedence rules of
.BR \-\-mood .
A script operand always does so.
.TP
.B \-\-dumb
Apply
.BR "\-\-mood sh" ,
.BR \-\-no\-completion ,
.BR "\-\-tab\-selector plain" ,
and
.BR \-\-no\-diagnostics ,
and disable color. An explicit
.B \-\-tab\-selector
overrides the plain selector.
.SS Auxiliary options
.TP
.B \-\-lint
Analyze the command strings, the file operands, or standard input without
running them, as described under
.BR LINTING .
.TP
.B \-\-format
Format one file or standard input to standard output, as described under
.BR FORMATTING .
Command strings are ignored, and more than one file needs
.BR \-\-apply .
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Update the file operands with the safe lint fixes under
.BR \-\-lint ,
the formatted source under
.BR \-\-format ,
or both. It needs one of them and at least one file. Standard input,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-c ,
and a lone
.B \-
are rejected.
.TP
.B \-\-as\-language\-server
Run the language server, as described under
.BR "LANGUAGE SERVER" .
File operands,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-\-lint ,
.BR \-\-format ,
and
.B \-\-apply
are rejected.
.SS Koshka options
.TP
.BR \-Q ", " \-\-no\-init\-files
Read no startup file, and set PATH to /usr/bin:/bin.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Read no settings file, and ignore and remove KOSHCONF. PATH and the startup
files are unchanged.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-WW ", " \-WWW
Set the analysis level. Analysis has strict, lenient, and annoying tiers. In
the
.B kosh
mood, strict and lenient findings are errors and annoying findings are
warnings.
.B \-W
keeps that,
.B \-WW
demotes lenient errors to warnings, and
.B \-WWW
also demotes strict errors. In the other moods no analysis runs until a level
is set, and
.BR \-W ,
.BR \-WW ,
and
.B \-WWW
enable the strict, the lenient, and the annoying tier as warnings, each with
the tiers before it. A level set with
.B set
passes to launched shells through
.BR KOSH_ANALYSIS .
Runtime diagnostics do not depend on the level, except that the compatibility
moods warn about an unset variable that expands to nothing only when a level
is set.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-diagnostics
List the analysis checks and their metadata, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
Skip analysis and every warning and pre-run diagnostic.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics
Suppress the annoying tier.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-init\-diagnostics
Suppress diagnostics and warnings while startup files run, and restore them
afterwards unless a startup file changed the setting.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-traces
Suppress source backtraces (optimizer.show_source_traces off).
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-no\-completion
Disable tab completion, ghost text, and syntax coloring (completion.on_tab
off).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-syntax\-highlighting
Disable syntax coloring and ghost text and keep tab completion
(editor.highlight_syntax_and_show_ghost_text off).
.TP
.BR \-\-tab\-selector " \fIMODE\fR"
Select how an explicit Tab presents several candidates and how Ctrl\-R presents
the history. MODE is
.BR interactive ,
the default,
.BR external ,
or
.BR plain .
See
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-koshkit
Resolve bundled utilities directly as commands, after PATH, as
.B koshconf set interpreter.resolve_koshkit_applets_as_commands on
does. The option is off by default and affects only the compatibility moods,
since the
.B kosh
mood always resolves bundled utilities after PATH.
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-extended\-arithmetic
Enable arbitrary-precision integers and finite decimal values, as
.B koshconf set interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers on
does. The
.B kosh
mood keeps this on.
.SS Debug options
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-show\-ast
Print syntax trees before execution and during formatting or linting.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-optimizer\-diagnostics
Report each constant-folded expression and eliminated branch as a warning. A
compatibility mood shows these warnings only under
.BR \-WW .
.TP
.B \-\-show\-exit\-code
Print a located diagnostic when a command returns a nonzero status
(debug.report_nonzero_exit_codes).
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-show\-all\-exit\-codes
Report every command status, including zero.
.B set \-N
and
.B set +N
toggle it.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-show\-lexed\-words
Print the escape map of each parsed command.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-stats
Print evaluation counts, arena use, the counts of shell variables, functions,
builtins, forks, and execs, the bytes and calls read and written, and resource
usage after each command.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-memory
Print the memory of the syntax tree arena, the function arenas, and the heap
at exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-ftrace ", " \-\-strace
Enable function or system-call tracing in a Cosmopolitan build.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I script
When neither
.B \-s
nor
.B \-c
selects another source, the first operand is the script file. A lone
.B \-
selects standard input. A directory fails with status 126, and a file that
cannot be opened fails with status 127.
.TP
.I argument
For a script, the script path becomes $0 and later arguments become positional
parameters. For
.BR \-c ,
the first operand after COMMAND becomes $0. For
.B \-s
and interactive operation, $0 stays the invocation name and every operand
becomes a positional parameter.
.SH MOODS
The
.B kosh
mood is the default. It enables strict checks and analysis and holds nounset,
pipefail, failglob, and extended arithmetic on and nullglob off.
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
and
.B koshconf
reject any other value of these with a hint to use
.BR "koshconf set mood bash" ,
but accept the value already held, so
.B set \-euo pipefail
still runs. Every other option can be toggled. The
.B kosh
mood also resolves bundled utilities as commands after PATH.
.PP
The
.B bash
mood accepts Bash extensions with analysis off by default. The
.B bash-posix
mood is Bash syntax with the POSIX option on, and sources the Bash startup
files. The
.B sh
mood follows dash. In this manual the
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods are the Bash moods, the
.B bash-posix
and
.B sh
moods, where the posix option is on, are the POSIX moods, and every mood
other than
.B kosh
is a compatibility mood. In the compatibility moods every Bash option can be
toggled, and an
explicit
.BR "set \-u" ,
.BR "set \-o pipefail" ,
.BR "shopt \-s failglob" ,
or
.B koshconf set interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers
survives a later change between them.
.B set \-o posix
selects
.BR bash-posix ,
and
.B set +o posix
selects
.B bash
from the
.B bash-posix
or
.B sh
mood.
.PP
The options whose defaults differ between the moods are these.
.PP
.nf
.ft CR
option               kosh  bash             bash-posix, sh
expand_aliases       on    off in a script  on
extglob              on    off              off
failglob             on    off              off
nounset              on    off              off
pipefail             on    off              off
extended arithmetic  on    off              off
posix                off   off              on
.ft R
.fi
.PP
In the
.B bash
mood, expand_aliases is on in an interactive shell. Every other option has the
same default in every mood. The moods also differ in grammar, expansion,
builtins, and errors, as the sections below describe.
.SH OPTION REGISTRY
Shell settings can be read and changed through
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
and
.BR koshconf ,
and are reported by the
.B \-o
test, SHELLOPTS, and BASHOPTS. Each setting has a numeric id and a koshconf
name. Some also have a Bash name or an option letter. Interactive settings
affect the editor, history, or diagnostic presentation. Semantic settings
can change script behavior.
.PP
The first component of a koshconf name is its topic. The
.B mood
and
.B init_moods
options select moods,
.B interpreter
covers shell behavior,
.B compatibility
covers Windows path mapping,
.B editor
covers the line editor and holds the
.B editor.history
and
.B editor.hints
groups,
.B optimizer
covers analysis, and
.B completion
and
.B debug
cover completion and debugging output. The name of an option from POSIX
.B set \-o
starts with posix, and one from another Bash
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
option starts with bash, as in posix.interpreter.exit_on_command_failure for
errexit. Every other option exists only in Koshka.
.PP
An option with a Bash spelling is also accepted as posix.legacy.NAME when POSIX
.B set \-o
accepts NAME, and as bash.legacy.NAME otherwise, as in bash.legacy.extglob.
editor.base_mode has the alias posix.legacy.vi only. The
alias is the same option, and every option operand and kosh.conf line accepts
it. Error messages, KOSHCONF,
.BR "koshconf create" ,
and
.B koshconf list
use only the canonical name, and
.B koshconf list \-\-all
adds each alias. The alias of interactive_comments is
bash.legacy.interactive\-comments, spelled as the
.B set \-o
option.
.PP
A settings file may set every option except those fixed by the invocation or
the session. bash.interpreter.login_shell and bash.interpreter.restricted_shell
are read-only. bash.interpreter.privileged_mode,
posix.interpreter.parse_without_executing, and
bash.interpreter.exit_after_one_command come from the command line.
posix.interpreter.job_control, bash.editor.history.records_commands,
bash.editor.history.bang_expansion, and bash.interpreter.posixly_correct follow
the session and the mood.
.PP
editor.base_mode covers the Bash options emacs and vi. It is vi while
.B set \-o vi
is on and emacs otherwise, and
.BR "set \-o emacs" ,
.BR "set \-o vi" ,
the
.B \-o
test, and SHELLOPTS behave as in Bash. The Bash options hashall and nolog have
no koshconf name.
.B set
and
.B \-o
accept them, and
.B koshconf list \-\-all
shows them as posix.legacy.hashall and posix.legacy.nolog. koshconf get,
koshconf set, and the settings files reject these spellings. The bash.legacy
shopt options in the table change nothing, so Bash startup files that set
them run unchanged. shopt accepts
array_expand_once as the Bash 5.3 spelling of assoc_expand_once.
.PP
In the table, i marks an interactive option and s a semantic one.
