188 s bash.legacy.bash_source_fullpath
.ft R
.fi
.PP
mood takes kosh, sh, bash, or bash-posix. completion.menu_style takes
interactive, external, or plain. optimizer.warning_level takes 0 to 3.
editor.base_mode takes emacs or vi. editor.history.file_path and
editor.history.max_entries correspond to KOSH_HISTORY_FILE and
KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE. editor.wrap_width is an integer from 0 to 65535, 80 by
default. init_moods is a comma-separated list of the moods
.B \-L
accepts, empty by default, which sources the startup files of the session mood
alone. It applies only to an interactive or login shell at startup,
.B \-L
replaces it, a restricted shell ignores it in the settings files, and KOSHCONF
never carries it.
.SH STARTUP FILES
Every shell applies the settings files before any startup file. They are the
system file /etc/kosh.conf (%ProgramData%\ekosh\ekosh.conf on Windows), then
the user file $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf or ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf,
then a
.B KOSHCONF
variable. The shell removes KOSHCONF from the environment after it applies it.
These files hold settings, and
.BR kosh (5)
describes them. A mood set there selects the startup files below unless the
command line or the invocation name selects one, and options given on the
command line keep their values. The superuser on POSIX has no user settings
file and no rc script.
.PP
A login or interactive shell in the
.B kosh
mood sources the rc script, $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh or
~/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh, and no system rc file. In an interactive
non-login shell,
.BR \-\-norc ,
.BR \-\-rcfile ,
and privileged mode apply to it as they apply to ~/.bashrc. A Bash startup file
that the script sources runs in the
.B bash
mood, as described under
.BR source .
.B \-L
on the command line, or init_moods in a settings file, replaces the list of
moods whose startup files run, so
.B \-L bash
makes a
.B kosh
session source only the Bash startup files, in the
.B bash
mood, and then return to the
.B kosh
mood, and
.B \-L bash,kosh
also sources the rc script afterwards.
.PP
A login shell in the
.B sh
mood sources /etc/profile and ~/.profile. An interactive
.B sh
shell sources the file in ENV, without a PATH search, unless privileged mode is
on.
.PP
A login shell in the
.B bash
or
.B bash-posix
mood sources /etc/profile, then the first readable file of ~/.bash_profile,
~/.bash_login, and ~/.profile. An interactive non-login shell in those moods
sources the first readable file of /etc/bash/bashrc and /etc/bash.bashrc, then
the custom rc file or ~/.bashrc. When it has sourced one of them, tab
completion is on, no default completion spec exists, and
BASH_COMPLETION_VERSINFO is unset, the shell then sources
/usr/share/bash-completion/bash_completion. A non-interactive
shell in the
.B bash
mood, including a login shell after its profiles and a
.B kosh
session started with
.BR "\-L bash" ,
sources BASH_ENV once, without a PATH search.
.B bash-posix
does not. A custom rc file applies only to an interactive non-login shell. On
Windows these files keep their POSIX paths.
.PP
Clean
.RB ( \-Q ),
rescue, lint, format, and language server shells skip every startup and
settings file, as does a shell whose real and effective ids differ.
.B \-\-no\-config
and privileged mode skip the settings files and KOSHCONF. A subshell receives
its settings from its parent, and an executable started as a bundled utility
or as koshkit applies none. Privileged
mode suppresses ENV, BASH_ENV, and the rc script of an interactive non-login
.B kosh
mood shell. Restricted mode, nounset, pipefail, and analysis take effect after
startup, but set, shopt, and koshconf in a startup file already reject a value
that the mood does not hold. A missing startup file is skipped, and an
unreadable custom rc or BASH_ENV file is also reported.
.SH DEFINITIONS
.TP
.B blank
A space or a tab.
.TP
.B word
A sequence of characters that the shell treats as one unit, also called a
token.
.TP
.B identifier
A word of letters, digits, and underscores that does not begin with a digit.
Variables and, in most contexts, functions are identifiers.
.TP
.B metacharacter
A character that separates words when it is unquoted. The metacharacters are a
blank, a newline, and | & ; ( ) < >.
.TP
.B control operator
A token that performs a control function. The control operators are a newline
and ||, &&, &, ;, ;;, ;&, ;;&, |, |&, (, and ).
.SH RESERVED WORDS
A reserved word has a special meaning when it is unquoted and appears as the
first word of a command, as the third word of a
.B case
or
.B for
command, or after another reserved word that starts a command list. The
reserved words are
.PP
.RS
! case coproc do done elif else esac fi for function if in select then time
until while { } [[ ]]
.RE
.PP
.B select
is reserved in the
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods only. The
.B sh
mood does not reserve
.BR coproc ,
.BR select ,
or
.BR time ,
and it reports [[ as a dialect error. A reserved word in any other position is
an ordinary word, so
.B echo if then fi
prints its arguments.
.SH SHELL GRAMMAR
The shell accepts the POSIX Shell Command Language and the Bash extensions
described in this manual. The
.B sh
mood follows dash. It reports [[ ]], the C-style for loop, |&, and <<< as
dialect errors, and a script holding one does not run. It does not recognize
$'...', $"...", brace expansion, &>, &>>, >&\fIfile\fR, (( )), $[ ], ${ }
function substitution, the ${\fIv\fR@\fIop\fR} transformations, or quotes
inside $(( )). It still accepts process substitution, the
.B function
keyword, and subscript assignment, and it assigns an empty string for a
compound assignment such as a=(x y).
.SS Simple commands
A simple command is a sequence of optional variable assignments followed by
words and redirections, ended by a control operator. The first word is the
command, and the remaining words are its arguments. The status of a simple
command is its exit status, or 128 plus the signal number when a signal ends
it.
.PP
An assignment before the command word applies to the environment of that
command only. The values of the assignments in one prefix are expanded before
any of them is made, so in
.B a=7 b=$a cmd
the variable b receives the previous value of a. After a special builtin, the
assignments remain in the shell in the
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash-posix ,
and
.B sh
moods, and they are removed in the
.B bash
mood. After a function call they are removed in every mood. A
command with assignments and no command word makes the assignments in the
shell.
.SS Pipelines
A pipeline is a sequence of one or more commands separated by | or |&, in the
following form.
.PP
.RS
[\fBtime\fR [\fB\-p\fR] [\fB\-R\fR]] [\fB!\fR] \fIcommand\fR [ { | | |& }
\fIcommand\fR ... ]
.RE
.PP
The standard output of each command is connected to the standard input of the
next. The form |& also connects standard error, as 2>&1 | does. Each command
runs in its own process, with these exceptions. In the
.B kosh
and
.B sh
moods, a builtin in the last stage runs in the shell, so
.B echo 5 | read x
sets x. In the
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods, the last stage runs in the shell only while the
.B lastpipe
option is on and job control is off. A function or a compound command in the
last stage runs in its own process in the
.B kosh
and
.B sh
moods.
.PP
The status of a pipeline is the status of its last command. While the
.B pipefail
option is on, it is the status of the rightmost command that failed, or 0. A
leading ! negates the status. PIPESTATUS holds the status of each stage of the
last foreground pipeline. A stage that expands to no command, such as
.BR "p=1 | cat" ,
is an error in the
.B kosh
mood, and the other moods apply its redirections and give it status 0.
.PP
The reserved word
.B time
reports the elapsed, user, and system time of the pipeline after it finishes.
In the
.B kosh
mood the report also has a processor usage row and, when it is nonzero and the
platform reports it, the peak resident set of the pipeline and its
descendants.
.B \-p
selects the POSIX format, which has no processor row, and
.B \-R
adds the resident set row in every format. TIMEFORMAT selects the format, as
described under
.BR PARAMETERS .
.B time
with no pipeline prints nothing. In the
.B sh
mood,
.B time
is an ordinary command found through PATH.
.SS Lists
A list is a sequence of pipelines separated by ;, &, &&, or ||, and optionally
ended by ;, &, or a newline. The operators && and || have equal precedence and
group from the left. They bind more tightly than ; and &, which also have equal
precedence.
.PP
.I command1
.B &&
.I command2
runs command2 only when command1 returns 0, and
.I command1
.B ||
.I command2
runs command2 only when command1 returns nonzero. The status of an and-or list
is the status of the last command that ran.
.PP
A list ended by ; or a newline runs in the foreground, and the shell waits for
it. An ampersand runs the whole and-or list before it asynchronously, so
.B a && b || c &
is one background job, and its variable and directory changes stay in the job.
The status of an asynchronous list is 0, and $! holds its process id. In the
Bash and
.B sh
moods, an asynchronous simple command starts its process before it expands its
words, assignments, and redirections, so an expansion error fails the job. The
status is 127 for an unset parameter or ${name?word} and 1 for a failed
redirection in the Bash moods, and 2 in the
.B sh
mood. The
.B kosh
mood expands the words before it starts the job.
.SS Compound commands
A compound command can be followed by redirections, which apply to the whole
command.
.TP
.BI ( list )
Run list in a subshell. Changes to variables, the working directory, options,
and traps do not reach the shell. The status is the status of list.
.TP
.BI "{ " list "; }"
Run list in the shell. The braces are reserved words, so they must be separate
words, and list must end with a semicolon or a newline.
.TP
.BI (( expression ))
Evaluate expression as described under
.BR "ARITHMETIC EVALUATION" .
The status is 0 when the value is nonzero, and 1 when the value is zero or the
expression is empty. The
.B sh
mood treats (( as two subshell openings.
.TP
.BI "[[ " expression " ]]"
Evaluate a conditional expression, as described under
.BR "CONDITIONAL EXPRESSIONS" .
The status is 0 or 1, and 2 when the expression cannot be evaluated.
.TP
\fBfor\fR \fIname\fR [ [\fBin\fR [\fIword\fR ...]] ; ] \fBdo\fR \fIlist\fR ; \fBdone\fR
Expand the words and run list once for each resulting field, with name set to
the field. Without
.BR in ,
the loop iterates over the positional parameters. The status is the status of
the last list that ran, or 0 when the loop did not iterate. The body must be a
.BR do " ... " done
list, and a brace group is rejected.
.TP
\fBfor ((\fR \fIexpr1\fR ; \fIexpr2\fR ; \fIexpr3\fR \fB))\fR ; \fBdo\fR \fIlist\fR ; \fBdone\fR
Evaluate expr1, then run list and evaluate expr3 as long as expr2 is nonzero.
An empty expression counts as 1. The
.B sh
mood reports this form as a dialect error.
.TP
\fBselect\fR \fIname\fR [\fBin\fR \fIword\fR ...] ; \fBdo\fR \fIlist\fR ; \fBdone\fR
Print the expanded words as a numbered menu on standard error, print PS3, and
read a line. A number of a word sets name to that word, any other line sets
name to empty, and REPLY holds the line. The body runs after each nonempty
line until a
.B break
or the end of input, and an empty line prints the menu again. Only the
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods accept select.
.TP
\fBcase\fR \fIword\fR \fBin\fR [ [(] \fIpattern\fR [ | \fIpattern\fR ] ... ) \fIlist\fR \fIterminator\fR ] ... \fBesac\fR
Expand word and run the list of the first pattern that matches it, as
described under
.BR "Pattern matching" .
The terminator ;; ends the command, ;& also runs the next list without testing
its pattern, and ;;& tests the next patterns. The status is the status of the
last list that ran, or 0 when no pattern matched. Every mood accepts the three
terminators.
.TP
\fBif\fR \fIlist\fR; \fBthen\fR \fIlist\fR; [ \fBelif\fR \fIlist\fR; \fBthen\fR \fIlist\fR; ] ... [ \fBelse\fR \fIlist\fR; ] \fBfi\fR
Run the then list of the first condition list that returns 0, or the else
list. The status is the status of the last list that ran, or 0 when no branch
ran.
.TP
\fBwhile\fR \fIlist\fR; \fBdo\fR \fIlist\fR; \fBdone\fR
.PD 0
.TP
\fBuntil\fR \fIlist\fR; \fBdo\fR \fIlist\fR; \fBdone\fR
.PD
Run the body as long as the condition list returns 0, or, for until, nonzero.
The status is the status of the last body that ran, or 0.
.SS Coprocesses
.EX
coproc [NAME] command [redirections]
.EE
.PP
Every mood except
.B sh
accepts a coprocess. The command runs asynchronously, connected to the shell by
two pipes. The shell receives the output of the coprocess from NAME[0] and
writes its input to NAME[1], and NAME_PID
holds the process id. NAME defaults to COPROC, and a word before the command
is NAME only when a compound command follows it. The launch returns status 0,
and $! holds
the process id. The descriptors are above 9, close across an exec, and are not
passed to subshells. When the coprocess finishes, the shell closes them and
unsets NAME and NAME_PID.
.SS Function definitions
.RS
\fIfname\fR () \fIcommand\fR [\fIredirections\fR]
.br
\fBfunction\fR \fIfname\fR [()] \fIcommand\fR [\fIredirections\fR]
.RE
.PP
A function definition stores command as the body of fname and returns 0. The
body can be any command, including a simple command, as in
.BR "f() echo hi" .
The redirections apply to every call. A later definition replaces the body. A
function made read-only with
.B readonly \-f
cannot be redefined. A function name can hold any characters that form one
unquoted word, such as f.g or f-g, and a quoted word also works as a name. A
reserved word after
.B function
is an error, and a name built by an expansion defines nothing. Calling a
function is described under
.BR FUNCTIONS .
.SH COMMENTS
An unquoted # at the start of a word begins a comment that lasts to the end of
the line. A # inside a word, as in a#b, is an ordinary character. An
interactive shell recognizes comments while the
.B interactive_comments
option is on. The option is on by default.
.SH QUOTING
Quoting removes the special meaning of characters and keeps a word from being
split or expanded.
.PP
An unquoted backslash preserves the next character, except that a backslash
before a newline joins the two lines. A backslash at the very end of the input
is an error in the
.B kosh
mood and a literal backslash in the other moods. On Windows an unquoted
backslash is also an escape, so a native path needs a quoted or doubled
backslash or a forward slash.
.PP
Single quotes preserve every enclosed character. A single quote cannot appear
between single quotes, even after a backslash.
.PP
Double quotes preserve every enclosed character except $, `, and \e. Parameter
expansion, command substitution, and arithmetic expansion take place inside
them. A backslash inside double quotes keeps its meaning only before $, `, ",
\e, or a newline. Inside double quotes, "$@" and "${a[@]}" still produce one
word per element.
.PP
A word of the form $'\fIstring\fR' expands backslash escapes and is otherwise
single-quoted. The escapes are \ea, \eb, \ee and \eE (escape), \ef, \en, \er,
\et, \ev, \e\e, \e', \e", \e?, \ex\fIH\fR and \ex\fIHH\fR (hexadecimal),
\e\fINNN\fR (one to three octal digits), \eu\fIHHHH\fR and
\eU\fIHHHHHHHH\fR (a Unicode character in UTF-8), and \ec\fIX\fR (a control
character). Any other escape stays as written, with its backslash. A NUL byte
ends the string.
.PP
A word of the form $"\fIstring\fR" marks the string for translation, and it
acts as a double-quoted string. The
.B sh
mood recognizes neither form and keeps the $ as an ordinary character.
.SH PARAMETERS
A parameter holds a value. A variable is a parameter denoted by an identifier,
a positional parameter is denoted by a number, and a special parameter by one
of the characters listed below. A variable is set when it has been assigned a
value, which may be empty, and it stays set until it is unset.
.PP
An assignment has the form
.IR name = value .
The value undergoes tilde expansion, parameter expansion, command
substitution, arithmetic expansion, and quote removal, but no field splitting
or pathname expansion. A tilde expands after the = and after each colon of the
value. The form
.IR name += value
appends to a string, adds to an integer, and appends elements to an array.
.B declare
and
.B local
give variables attributes, such as integer, read-only, exported, or a case
conversion, as described under
.BR BUILTINS .
.SS Positional parameters
The positional parameters are the arguments of the shell, a function, or a
sourced file, numbered from 1. A parameter above 9 needs braces, so $10 is $1
followed by a 0 and ${10} is the tenth parameter.
.B set \-\-
replaces them, and
.B shift
discards the first ones. They cannot be assigned directly, and ${@:=x} is an
error.
.PP
An unquoted $@ or $* is subject to field splitting. "$@" keeps one field per
parameter, and "$*" joins the parameters with the first byte of IFS. A $@ that
becomes one string, as in an assignment, joins with the first byte of IFS in
the
.B sh
mood and with a space in the Bash moods. In the
.B sh
mood, as in dash, $@ and $* are always set, so "${@\-d}" and "${@+p}" never
test for missing parameters. ${@:\fIoffset\fR:\fIlength\fR} selects parameters,
and an offset of 0 includes $0.
.SS Special parameters
.TP
.B *
The positional parameters, joined as described above.
.TP
.B @
The positional parameters, one field each inside double quotes.
.TP
.B #
The number of positional parameters.
.TP
.B ?
The status of the last command, pipeline, or command substitution.
.TP
.B \-
The letters of the options that are on, as described under
.BR OPTIONS .
The letters h and B are on by default, c appears for a command string, and u
appears in the
.B kosh
mood.
.TP
.B $
The process id of the shell. A subshell and a command substitution keep the
value of the shell that started them.
.TP
.B !
The process id of the last asynchronous command, coprocess, or process
substitution. It is empty before the first one.
.TP
.B 0
The name of the shell or the script, as described under
.BR OPERANDS .
Inside a function it stays the shell or script name.
.TP
.B _
The last argument of the previous simple command. It is empty at startup. An
assignment with no command and a [[ ]] command do not change it.
.SS Shell variables
The shell sets the following variables. The
.B sh
mood sets only PIPESTATUS, LINENO, OPTIND, PPID, SHLVL, PWD, OLDPWD, IFS, PS2,
PS4, and the KOSH and KOSHCONF variables among them. An assignment to a variable
that the shell maintains, such as BASHPID or HISTCMD, is discarded unless the
entry states otherwise. The shell ignores a value of such a variable inherited
from the environment, and children receive the inherited value unchanged.
.TP
.BR BASH ", " BASH_VERSION ", " BASH_VERSINFO
A Bash identity, set when the mood or an initialized mood is
.B bash
or
.BR bash-posix .
BASH holds the executable, BASH_VERSION is 5.3.0(1)\-kosh, and BASH_VERSINFO
holds the version fields, with the build host in element 5.
.TP
.B BASH_ALIASES
The aliases. Assigning an element defines an alias, unsetting an element does
nothing, and unsetting the array disconnects it.
.TP
.BR BASH_ARGC ", " BASH_ARGV
The argument count of each recorded frame, and the arguments in reverse order,
innermost first. The
.B extdebug
option records function and source calls in them. Without it BASH_ARGV is
empty and BASH_ARGC holds one 0. Assignments do nothing, and unset and local
fail.
.TP
.B BASH_ARGV0
Argument zero of the shell. An assignment does not change $0.
.TP
.B BASH_COMMAND
The command that is running or about to run.
.TP
.B BASH_EXECUTION_STRING
The command string of
.BR \-c .
.TP
.BR BASH_LINENO ", " BASH_SOURCE ", " FUNCNAME
The call stack. FUNCNAME[0] is the running function, BASH_SOURCE[i] is the
file that defines FUNCNAME[i], and BASH_LINENO[i] is the line where
FUNCNAME[i] was called. They are empty in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B BASH_MONOSECONDS
A monotonic clock in milliseconds.
.TP
.B BASH_REMATCH
The match and the groups of the last successful [[ =~ ]], and empty after a
failed match.
.TP
.B BASH_SUBSHELL
The subshell depth, 0 in the shell.
.TP
.B BASHOPTS
The read-only, colon-separated list of the
.B shopt
options that are on. A value in the environment is ignored.
.TP
.B BASHPID
The process id of the current shell or subshell.
.TP
.BR COMP_CWORD ", " COMP_LINE ", " COMP_POINT ", " COMP_WORDS
The cursor word index, the line, the cursor byte offset, and the words, for a
completion function, as described under
.BR complete .
.B COMP_KEY
and
.B COMP_TYPE
are exported to a
.B complete \-C
command only.
.TP
.BR COPROC ", " COPROC_PID
The descriptors and process of a coprocess without NAME.
.TP
.B DIRSTACK
The current directory followed by the saved stack. Assigning an existing index
other than 0 changes that entry, other assignments and element unsets do
nothing, and unsetting the array disconnects it.
.TP
.BR EPOCHREALTIME ", " EPOCHSECONDS
The wall time in seconds, with microseconds in EPOCHREALTIME.
.TP
.BR EUID ", " UID
The read-only effective and real user ids.
.TP
.B GROUPS
The real group id.
.TP
.B HISTCMD
The history number of the current command, 0 in a non-interactive shell.
.TP
.BR HOSTNAME ", " HOSTTYPE ", " MACHTYPE ", " OSTYPE
The host and the platform.
.TP
.B KOSH
The invocation path. Every mood sets it without exporting it, so a line
guarded by
.B test \-n \(dq$KOSH\(dq
runs only in Koshka.
.TP
.BR KOSH_VERSION ", " KOSH_COMMIT ", " KOSH_BUILD_MODE ", " KOSH_OS
The version, build commit, build mode, and build host. They are not exported.
.TP
.B KOSH_IDENTITY
The read-only, exported CRC-32 of the running executable, in lowercase hex.
.TP
.B KOSH_ANSI_*
Terminal escapes in BLACK, RED, GREEN, YELLOW, BLUE, MAGENTA, CYAN, WHITE, their
BRIGHT_ forms, BOLD, DIM, and RESET, empty when color is disabled. An
assignment replaces the value.
.TP
.B KOSH_ANALYSIS
The analysis settings changed with
.BR set ,
exported as a list of words. The word
.B mimicry
stands for
.BR \-I ,
.BI warnings= N
for the
.B \-W
level,
.B no\-annoying
for optimizer.show_annoying_tier off, and
.B no\-diagnostics
for optimizer.analyze_before_running off. Each
.B set
rewrites it, and it is removed when empty. A launched shell applies it, and an
explicit
.B \-W
replaces the inherited level. Command-line options do not set it.
.TP
.BR KOSH_GIT_BRANCH ", " KOSH_GIT_AHEAD ", " KOSH_GIT_BEHIND
The branch of the repository holding the working directory, a short hash for a
detached HEAD, or empty, and the unpushed and unpulled commit counts against
the upstream, empty when zero or unavailable. The search stops at
GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES. An assignment replaces the branch.
.TP
.B KOSH_PS1_*
The current text of each prompt escape, as described under
.BR PROMPTING .
.TP
.B KOSHCONF
The current mood and interactive options in the encoding of
.BR kosh (5).
Assignments are discarded, and the shell never exports it by itself.
.TP
.B LINENO
The line of the running command. Inside a command substitution that spans
several lines, each command reports its own line in the file. Assignments are
discarded, and unset keeps its meaning.
.TP
.B MAPFILE
The default array of
.BR mapfile .
.TP
.BR OLDPWD ", " PWD
The previous and current logical working directories, updated by
.BR cd .
They are exported only when they were inherited exported or are exported
explicitly. A logical PWD is accepted at startup only when it is absolute and
identifies the current directory.
.TP
.BR OPTARG ", " OPTIND
The argument of the last option and the index of the next argument for
.BR getopts .
OPTIND starts at 1.
.TP
.B PIPESTATUS
The status of each stage of the last foreground pipeline.
.TP
.B PPID
The process id of the parent of the shell. It is read-only except in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
.B RANDOM
A random integer from 0 to 32767. Assigning it seeds the sequence
reproducibly, and unsetting it makes it an ordinary variable.
.TP
.B REPLY
The line read by
.B read
without a variable, and the line read by
.BR select .
.TP
.B SECONDS
The seconds since the shell started. Assigning it moves its base, and unsetting
it makes it an ordinary variable.
.TP
.B SHELLOPTS
The read-only, colon-separated list of the
.B set \-o
options that are on. A value in the environment is ignored.
.TP
.B SHLVL
The nesting level of shells, incremented at startup. A negative inherited value
becomes 1, and an inherited value of 1000 or more restarts at 1.
.TP
.B SRANDOM
A random 32-bit integer from the system generator.
.PP
The following variables affect the shell.
.TP
.BR BASH_COMPLETION_VERSINFO
When set, an interactive
.B bash
mood shell does not source the bash-completion script.
.TP
.BR BASH_ENV ", " ENV
Startup files, as described under
.BR "STARTUP FILES" .
Their values undergo parameter expansion and command substitution.
.TP
.B BASH_XTRACEFD
The descriptor that receives
.B xtrace
output. Standard error receives it when the variable is unset or invalid.
.TP
.B CDPATH
The colon-separated directories that
.B cd
searches for a relative operand.
.TP
.BR COLUMNS ", " LINES
The terminal size, set at the start of an interactive shell. They are not
updated when the terminal changes size.
.TP
.BR FCEDIT ", " EDITOR ", " VISUAL
FCEDIT, then EDITOR, selects the editor of
.BR fc .
VISUAL, then EDITOR, selects the editor of Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E.
.TP
.B GLOBIGNORE
Colon-separated patterns of pathname expansion results to remove, as described
under
.BR "Pathname expansion" .
.TP
.B HOME
The home directory, used by
.B cd
and tilde expansion. On Windows the shell uses USERPROFILE when HOME is unset.
.TP
.B IFS
The field separators and the joiner of "$*", by default space, tab, and
newline. A value in the environment is ignored.
.TP
.B IGNOREEOF
The number of consecutive end-of-file inputs that an interactive shell needs
before it exits while
.B ignoreeof
is on. Enabling the option sets an unset IGNOREEOF to 10, assigning IGNOREEOF
enables the option, and unsetting it disables the option.
.TP
.BR KOSH_HISTORY_FILE ", " KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE
The history file and its size, as described under
.BR HISTORY .
.TP
.B KOSH_DIRECTORY_HISTORY
The file of visited directories that
.B z
ranks, ~/.kosh_directory_history by default.
.TP
.B KOSH_CALC_HISTORY
The history file of
.BR calc ,
~/.kosh_calc_history by default.
.TP
.B KOSH_FLAGS
Default command-line options, as described under
.BR INVOCATION .
.TP
.BR KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND ", " KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_OPTS
The external selector program, fzf by default, and its options, by default
\-\-multi \-\-layout=reverse \-\-height=~40% \-\-min\-height=3, split on blanks
without expansion.
.TP
.B KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION
A value of 0 turns off the terminal integration marks.
.TP
.BR KOSH_WELCOME ", " KOSH_FAREWELL
Messages printed with the shell prefix at interactive startup and exit. An
interactive shell sets them unless the environment holds them, to Welcome :3
in the
.B kosh
mood, Bash me harder! in the Bash moods, POSIX me harder! in the
.B sh
mood, and Goodbye :c. An exported empty value, or a value of zero display
width such as one set by printf \-v KOSH_WELCOME \(dq\er\(dq, disables a
message, and a startup file can set one, as in KOSH_WELCOME=$(date).
.TP
.BR LANG ", " LC_ALL ", " LC_COLLATE ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LC_NUMERIC
The locale, as described under
.BR LOCALE .
.TP
.B MANPATH
The manual page directories that completion, hints, and
.B man
search.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
A nonempty value disables color in the prompt, diagnostics, the editor, and the
bundled utilities.
.TP
.B OPTERR
A value of 0 silences
.B getopts
diagnostics.
.TP
.B PATH
The colon-separated command search path. An empty element, an empty value, and
an unset PATH search the current directory. The shell does not set PATH when
the environment lacks it. On Windows the separator is a semicolon, an empty
element is skipped, and Path counts as PATH.
.TP
.BR PROMPT_COMMAND ", " PS0 ", " PS1 ", " PS2 ", " PS3 ", " PS4 ", " RPS1 ", " RPROMPT
The prompts and the command run before each prompt, as described under
.BR PROMPTING .
PS3 is the prompt of
.BR select ,
"#? " by default.
.TP
.B SHELL
The path of the user shell. An inherited value is kept, and an unset SHELL is
set to the path of the executable.
.TP
.BR TERM ", " COLORTERM ", " TERM_PROGRAM
Terminal capabilities, as described under
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
TERM=dumb disables color and the terminal integration marks.
.TP
.B TIMEFORMAT
The format of the
.B time
report. %R, %U, and %S are the elapsed, user, and system seconds, %P is the
processor percentage, %3R selects three decimals, %lR selects minutes and
seconds, and %% is a percent sign. An empty value prints nothing, and an unset
value selects the default report.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
The directory for the temporary files of
.BR fc ,
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E, and
.BR slurp ,
read from the environment, so an assignment needs
.BR export .
An empty value is ignored, and /tmp is the default. On Windows the directory is
TEMP.
.TP
.B TZ
The time zone. The shell uses the value it inherited, so an assignment in the
shell changes the zone of later programs only.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The base of the user settings directory, as described under
.BR "STARTUP FILES" .
A relative or empty value is ignored.
.PP
These Bash variables have no effect. They are HISTCONTROL, HISTFILE,
HISTFILESIZE, HISTIGNORE, HISTSIZE, HISTTIMEFORMAT, histchars, MAIL, MAILCHECK,
MAILPATH, TMOUT, PROMPT_DIRTRIM, FIGNORE, EXECIGNORE, HOSTFILE, INPUTRC,
CHILD_MAX, BASH_COMPAT, BASH_LOADABLES_PATH, GLOBSORT, FUNCNEST, auto_resume,
and POSIXLY_CORRECT.
.SS Arrays
An indexed array is a list of values with integer indices from 0, and an
associative array maps string keys to values. In the
.B sh
mood a subscript assignment creates an element, a compound assignment assigns
an empty string, and unset rejects a subscript.
.PP
a=(x y z) assigns an indexed array, a=([2]=c [0]=a) assigns explicit indices,
a[2]=z assigns an element, and a+=(w) appends. Elements may be missing, so
a[5]=z on a two-element array leaves indices 0, 1, and 5. ${a[i]} takes an
arithmetic subscript, and a negative index counts from the end. A scalar
variable acts as element 0, and ${a} is ${a[0]}. ${a[@]} and ${a[*]} list
elements, ${#a[@]} counts them, and ${!a[@]} lists the indices that are set.
"${a[@]}" keeps one field per element, "${a[@]@A}" yields the words of the
declaration, "${a[@]@K}" yields keys and values, and "${a[*]}" joins with the
first byte of IFS. An unquoted ${a[@]} or ${a[*]} joins with the first byte of
IFS and splits the result. Under
.BR "set \-u" ,
${a[@]} and ${a[*]} of an unset array expand to nothing, and $a and ${a[0]}
are errors.
.B unset 'a[i]'
removes one element.
.PP
.B declare \-A m
declares an associative array, m[key]=v assigns an element, ${m[key]} expands
it, ${!m[@]} lists keys, and
.B unset 'm[key]'
removes one. A key is a string, so m[1+1] holds the key 1+1. Keys are listed in
the order in which they were first assigned. Bash can list them in another
order, so a script that depends on the order should sort the keys.
.PP
.B export
and
.B declare \-x
on an array keep the array and record the export attribute.
.B declare \-p
prints the attribute as \-ax or \-Ax, and no child receives the array.
.SH EXPANSIONS
The shell expands each word of a command in a fixed order. Brace expansion
comes first, then tilde expansion, parameter expansion, arithmetic expansion,
command substitution, and process substitution from left to right, and then
field splitting, pathname expansion, and quote removal. Only brace expansion,
field splitting, and pathname expansion can change the number of words.
.SS Brace expansion
A word holding an unquoted {\fIa\fR,\fIb\fR,...} produces one word per item,
each with the text before and after the braces, as in a{b,c}d giving abd and
acd. Items may be empty and braces nest. A sequence {\fIx\fR..\fIy\fR} or
{\fIx\fR..\fIy\fR..\fIstep\fR} produces integers or single characters from x to
y. A leading zero on either integer pads every result to the same width, a
step of 0 counts as 1, and the sign of the step is ignored. A character range
includes every character between, as in {Z..a}. A word that is not a valid
list or sequence, such as {a}, {a\-z}, or {1..a}, stays literal, and so does a
sequence that holds an expansion. Brace expansion does not apply in an
assignment value, a here-string, a case pattern, or a redirection target. It
is off in the
.B sh
mood and while
.B braceexpand
is off.
.SS Tilde expansion
An unquoted ~ at the start of a word expands to HOME, ~\fIuser\fR to the home
directory of user, ~+ to PWD, and ~\- to OLDPWD. The prefix ends at the first
slash. An unknown user, ~\- with OLDPWD unset, and the directory stack forms ~N,
~+N, and ~\-N stay literal. An empty HOME expands to an empty word, and an
unset HOME is an error. A tilde also expands after = and : in an assignment and
in the words of
.BR export ,
.BR declare ,
.BR local ,
and
.BR readonly ,
and outside the
.B sh
mood in any argument that looks like an assignment, such as a=~.
.SS Parameter expansion
$\fIname\fR and ${\fIname\fR} expand to the value of a parameter. The braces are
needed for a positional parameter above 9, an array element, or a name
followed by a character that could continue it. In the forms below, a colon
also tests for an empty value, and without the colon only an unset parameter
counts. The word is expanded only when it is used.
.TP
.BI ${ name :\- word }
The value, or word when the parameter is unset or empty.
.TP
.BI ${ name := word }
The value, after assigning word when the parameter is unset or empty.
Positional and special parameters cannot be assigned this way.
.TP
.BI ${ name :? word }
The value, or an error message holding word when the parameter is unset or
empty. A non-interactive shell exits.
.TP
.BI ${ name :+ word }
Word when the parameter is set and not empty, and nothing otherwise.
.TP
.BI ${# name }
The length of the value in characters. ${#*} and ${#@} give the number of
positional parameters.
.TP
.BI ${ name : offset } ", " ${ name : offset : length }
A substring. A negative offset counts from the end and needs a leading space,
as in ${v: \-3}, since ${v:\-3} is the default-value form. A negative length
excludes that many characters from the end. On an indexed array,
${a[@]:offset:length} starts at the first element whose index is at least the
offset.
.TP
.BI ${ name # pattern } ", " ${ name ## pattern }
The value with the shortest or the longest prefix matching pattern removed.
.TP
.BI ${ name % pattern } ", " ${ name %% pattern }
The value with the shortest or the longest suffix matching pattern removed.
.TP
.BI ${ name / pattern / string }
The value with the first match of pattern replaced by string. // replaces every
match, and /# and /% anchor the match at the start and the end. The
.B patsub_replacement
option is on by default. While it is on, an unquoted ampersand in the
replacement, or one
from an unquoted expansion, stands for the match, and a quoted ampersand or \e&
is literal. With the option off, every ampersand is literal.
.TP
.BI ${ name ^ pattern } ", " ${ name ^^ pattern } ", " ${ name , pattern } ", " ${ name ,, pattern } ", " ${ name ~ pattern } ", " ${ name ~~ pattern }
Convert the first character, or every character, that matches pattern to
upper case, lower case, or the other case. Without a pattern, every character
matches.
.TP
.BI ${ name @ op }
Transform the value. Q quotes it for reuse, E expands backslash escapes as
$'...' does, P expands it as a prompt, A gives an assignment or declaration, a
gives the attribute letters, U, L, and u convert to upper case, lower case, or
an upper-case first character, and K and k give the keys and values of an
array. Any other operator on a set parameter is a bad substitution. The
.B sh
mood has no transformations.
.TP
.BI ${! name }
The value of the variable whose identifier is the value of name. For a name
reference it gives the identifier of the target. An unset or empty name gives
an empty value.
.TP
.BI ${! prefix *} ", " ${! prefix @}
The identifiers of the variables whose identifiers begin with prefix.
.TP
.BI ${! name [@]} ", " ${! name [*]}
The indices or keys of an array.
.PP
A value or key with a byte that does not print in the locale, such as a
control byte or invalid UTF-8, takes the $'...' form in ${v@Q}, ${v@A},
${a[@]@K}, printf %q, declare \-p, and export \-p.
.PP
Inside double quotes or a here-document, the word of ${v:\-word}, ${v:+word},
and ${v:=word}, with or without the colon, follows double-quote rules, so
"${v:\-'a'}" expands to 'a' with its quotes.
.SS Command substitution
$(\fIcommand\fR) and `\fIcommand\fR` expand to the output of the command with
trailing newlines removed. Inside backquotes, a backslash keeps its meaning
only before $, `, or \e. $(< \fIfile\fR) expands to the contents of file
without running a command. The Bash and
.B sh
moods drop null bytes from the output, and the
.B kosh
mood keeps them. The command runs in a subshell, so the shell state, options,
directory stack, working directory, and file creation mask of the shell are
unchanged after it finishes.
.PP
The
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash ,
and
.B bash-posix
moods also accept two forms that run in the shell. ${ \fIcommand\fR; } expands
to the output of the command, and ${| \fIcommand\fR; } expands to the value of
REPLY after the command. Variable changes in both forms remain.
.PP
A syntax error inside a substitution is reported at its place in the outer
source, and the script or command string holding it does not run. Nesting
deeper than 64 levels is a syntax error.
.SS Arithmetic expansion
$((\fIexpression\fR)) expands to the value of the expression, evaluated as
described under
.BR "ARITHMETIC EVALUATION" .
The expression undergoes parameter expansion, command substitution, and quote
removal first. An empty $(( )) yields 0. Outside the
.B sh
mood, $[\fIexpression\fR] is an obsolete spelling of the same expansion.
.SS Process substitution
<(\fIcommand\fR) and >(\fIcommand\fR) expand to a path from which the output
of the command can be read, or to which its input can be written, with the
command in a separate process.
The form expands anywhere in an unquoted word, so p=<(command) assigns the
path, and every mood accepts it. The path stays open until the command that
expanded it finishes, or until the loop finishes for a path in the word list of
a for loop. The shell then closes it and waits for the process. A process
substitution sets $! to its process, and
.B wait
on that process returns its status. On POSIX the path is /dev/fd/63 or the next
free descriptor above it. On Windows it is a pipe that acts as a FIFO, so each
reader continues where the previous one stopped, and each writer appends to the
same stream.
.SS Field splitting
The results of unquoted parameter expansion, command substitution, and
arithmetic expansion are split into fields at the bytes of IFS. A run of IFS
whitespace collapses and is ignored at the start and the end, and each other
IFS byte delimits a field, and that field may be empty. An empty IFS disables
splitting.
An unquoted expansion that yields nothing produces no field, and "" produces an
empty field.
.SS Pathname expansion
After field splitting, each unquoted word holding *, ?, or [ is a pattern, and
the shell replaces it with the sorted list of matching pathnames, as described
under
.BR "Pattern matching" .
A leading dot and a slash must match explicitly, and . and .. are never
results. The Bash moods sort matches by the collation order of the locale, and
the
.B kosh
and
.B sh
moods by byte value.
.B noglob
disables pathname expansion.
.PP
.B dotglob
lets * and ? match a leading dot,
.B nocaseglob
ignores case, and
.B globstar
makes ** match any number of directories, with **/ matching directories only.
A pattern that matches nothing is an error in the
.B kosh
mood. In the other moods it stays literal,
.B nullglob
removes it, and
.B failglob
makes it an error.
.PP
A nonempty
.B GLOBIGNORE
holds colon-separated patterns. A result matching one is removed, and a
pattern whose results are all removed follows the rules for a pattern that
matches nothing. Assigning a nonempty GLOBIGNORE enables
.BR dotglob ,
and unsetting it disables
.BR dotglob .
.SS Pattern matching
Pathname expansion, case, the == and != operators of [[ ]], and the pattern
forms of parameter expansion use these patterns. * matches any string, ?
matches one character, and [...] matches one character from a set. A set may
hold ranges such as a\-z and the classes [:alnum:], [:alpha:], [:blank:],
[:cntrl:], [:digit:], [:graph:], [:lower:], [:print:], [:punct:], [:space:],
[:upper:], and [:xdigit:]. A leading ! or ^ negates the set, and ] first in the
set is literal. Equivalence classes and collating symbols match nothing, and so
do reversed ranges. An unclosed [ is literal. A backslash or quotes make a
character literal. Outside pathname expansion, * and ? also match a slash and a
leading dot.
.PP
While
.B extglob
is on, ?(\fIlist\fR), *(\fIlist\fR), +(\fIlist\fR), @(\fIlist\fR), and
!(\fIlist\fR) match zero or one, zero or more, one or more, exactly one, or
anything except one of the |-separated patterns. The pattern of [[ ]] always
accepts them.
.B nocasematch
makes case, [[ ]], and =~ ignore case.
.SS Quote removal
After the other expansions, unquoted backslashes, single quotes, and double
quotes that did not result from an expansion are removed.
.SH REDIRECTIONS
A redirection opens, duplicates, or closes a descriptor for a command.
Redirections are processed from left to right. A number before the operator
selects the descriptor, and a {\fIvariable\fR} before it allocates a
descriptor numbered 10 or above and stores the number in the variable. The
variable may be an array element, and the number stays in the variable after the
descriptor closes. The target word undergoes the same expansions as an
argument except pathname expansion of more than one result. A target that
expands to several words is an error, and so is an unset variable in the
.B kosh
mood.
.PP
A failed redirection fails the command with status 1 in the Bash moods and 2
in the
.B kosh
and
.B sh
moods. On a special builtin or a compound command it ends the script in the
.B kosh
mood with status 1 and in the
.B sh
mood with status 2, and the Bash moods continue, including
.BR bash-posix ,
where Bash exits.
.TP
.RI [ n ] < word
Open word for reading on n, by default 0.
.TP
.RI [ n ] > word
Open word for writing on n, by default 1, creating it or truncating it. While
.B noclobber
is on, an existing regular file is refused.
.TP
.RI [ n ] >| word
Open word for writing, even under
.BR noclobber .
.TP
.RI [ n ] >> word
Open word for appending. An appending redirection inside a loop body opens its
file once for the whole loop, so writes continue to the original file even
after it is renamed or removed.
.TP
.RI [ n ] <> word
Open word for reading and writing on n, by default 0.
.TP
.RI &> word ", " >& word ", " &>> word
Redirect standard output and standard error to word, or append both. With
>&, a word that is a number, or \-, duplicates or closes standard output, and
&> and &>> always open a file.
.TP
.RI [ n ] <& m ", " [ n ] >& m
Duplicate descriptor m onto n. A target that is not a single open descriptor
is an error.
.TP
.RI [ n ] <&\- ", " [ n ] >&\-
Close n.
.TP
.RI [ n ] <& m \- ", " [ n ] >& m \-
Move m onto n and close m.
.TP
.RI [ n ] << word
A here-document. The lines after the command, up to a line that is exactly
word, become the input. When any part of word is quoted, the lines are taken
literally. Otherwise they undergo parameter expansion, command substitution,
and arithmetic expansion, a backslash keeps its meaning only before $, `, \e,
or a newline, and a backslash before a newline joins lines. A command can have
several here-documents, read in order. An unterminated here-document ends at
the end of the input.
.TP
.RI [ n ] <<\- word
A here-document whose lines and terminator lose their leading tabs.
.TP
.RI [ n ] <<< word
A here-string. The expanded word, followed by a newline, becomes the input.
.PP
The
.B sh
mood rejects &>, &>>, >&\fIword\fR, and <<<. Device paths such as /dev/tcp
are not emulated, so a redirection to one opens the path in the file system.
.PP
In the Bash moods, the redirections of a program, of a command with no command
word, and of a subshell expand in the child, so a side effect such as
${v:=x} does not reach the shell. Those of a builtin, a function, a group, and
.B exec
expand in the shell, as do those of every command in the
.B kosh
and
.B sh
moods.
.SH ALIASES
An alias replaces the first word of a simple command with a string. The
replacement is expanded when the command runs, so an alias defined earlier on
the same line or in the same compound command applies at once, and an alias in
a function body takes its value at each call. When the replacement ends with a
blank, the next word is also checked for an alias. A chain of aliases is
followed, and a word that is already being replaced is not replaced again. A
quoted word, a word with a backslash, and a word produced by
an expansion are never aliases, and a reserved word keeps its meaning.
.PP
Aliases expand while
.B expand_aliases
is on. It is on in the
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash-posix ,
and
.B sh
moods, and in the
.B bash
mood it is on in an interactive shell and off in a script.
.B alias
and
.B unalias
manage them, and BASH_ALIASES holds them.
.SH FUNCTIONS
A function runs in the shell, with its own positional parameters. Its
variables are global unless
.B local
or
.B declare
makes them local, and a local variable is visible to the functions it calls.
In the
.B kosh
and
.B sh
moods, local without a value keeps the outer value visible, and in the Bash
moods the variable starts unset.
.B return
ends the function with a status, and otherwise the status is that of the last
command. FUNCNAME, BASH_SOURCE, BASH_LINENO, and BASH_COMMAND expose the call
stack, and $0 stays the script name. Function calls nest at most 900 deep and
sourced files 400 deep, or less when the stack runs short, and a deeper call
fails with a diagnostic.
.PP
A function called from a guarded and-or operand or a negation keeps
.B errexit
suppressed throughout its body. In the
.B kosh
mood the RETURN trap fires at the end of every function body. The
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods fire it for a body that
.B functrace
follows, and the
.B sh
mood rejects the RETURN condition.
.B export \-f
exports a function to the environment as BASH_FUNC_\fIname\fR%%, which Bash
children import. Koshka does not import exported functions.
.SH ARITHMETIC EVALUATION
Arithmetic expressions appear in $(( )), (( )), let, the C-style for loop,
array subscripts, substring offsets, and assignments to integer variables. The
operators, from the highest precedence to the lowest, are
.PP
.RS
.nf
\fIid\fR++ \fIid\fR\-\-        post-increment, post-decrement
++\fIid\fR \-\-\fIid\fR        pre-increment, pre-decrement
\- +              unary minus, unary plus
! ~              logical and bitwise negation
**               exponentiation, grouping from the right
* / %            multiplication, division, remainder
+ \-              addition, subtraction
<< >>            shifts
<= >= < >        comparisons
== !=            equality
&                bitwise and
^                bitwise exclusive or
|                bitwise or
&&               logical and
||               logical or
\fIa\fR ? \fIb\fR : \fIc\fR        conditional
= *= /= %= += \-= <<= >>= &= ^= |= **=
                 assignment
,                sequence
.fi
.RE
.PP
Parentheses group. A comparison or logical operator yields 1 or 0, and && and
|| evaluate their right side only when needed. **= is a Koshka extension.
.PP
A number is decimal, octal with a leading 0, hexadecimal with 0x, binary with
0b, which is a Koshka extension, or in base \fIb\fR from 2 to 64 as
\fIb\fR#\fIdigits\fR, with digits beyond
9 taken from letters, @, and _. A variable is replaced by its value, and the
value is evaluated as an expression in turn, so x=1+2 makes x*2 equal 6.
An empty value counts as 0. A $ or a backquote inside a variable value is an
error. A subscript taken from a value is expanded again, so with q='a[$i]',
$((q)) is the element at i. Outside the
.B sh
mood, double quotes inside $(( )), (( )), a C-style for header, a subscript,
or a substring offset are removed first, so $(( "1 + 2" * 3 )) is 7. The
.B sh
mood rejects every quote there.
.PP
In the
.BR bash ,
.BR bash-posix ,
and
.B sh
moods, arithmetic uses signed 64-bit integers that wrap, and an unset variable
counts as 0. The
.B interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers
option, held on in the
.B kosh
mood, selects arbitrary-precision integers and finite decimal values, so
$((2**100)) is exact and $((7.0/2)) is 3.5. Division of two integers stays
integer division. Shifts, bitwise operators, and the exponent need integers,
and a negative shift count is an error. In the
.B kosh
mood an unset variable in an expression is an error.
.PP
Division by zero, an invalid digit, a missing operand, and an unexpected token
are errors, as described under
.BR "SHELL ERRORS" .
.SH CONDITIONAL EXPRESSIONS
The [[ ]] command and the
.B test
and [ builtins evaluate conditional expressions. Inside [[ ]] the words do not
undergo field splitting or pathname expansion. The primaries are
.TP
.BI \-a " file" ", " \-e " file"
The file exists. [[ ]] accepts \-a as a unary operator, and test does not.
.TP
.BI \-b " file" ", " \-c " file" ", " \-d " file" ", " \-f " file" ", " \-p " file" ", " \-S " file"
The file is a block device, a character device, a directory, a regular file, a
FIFO, or a socket.
.TP
.BI \-h " file" ", " \-L " file"
The file is a symbolic link.
.TP
.BI \-g " file" ", " \-u " file" ", " \-k " file"
The file has the set-group-id, set-user-id, or sticky bit.
.TP
.BI \-r " file" ", " \-w " file" ", " \-x " file"
The file is readable, writable, or executable by the shell.
.TP
.BI \-O " file" ", " \-G " file"
The file is owned by the effective user or group.
.TP
.BI \-s " file"
The file is a regular file with a nonzero size. A directory is false.
.TP
.BI \-t " fd"
The descriptor is open on a terminal. [ \-t ] with no operand is true.
.TP
.BI \-z " string" ", " \-n " string"
The string is empty, or not empty. A lone string is true when it is not empty.
.TP
.BI \-v " name"
The variable, array element, or positional parameter is set. Only [[ ]] accepts
\-v.
.TP
.BI \-R " name"
The variable is a name reference with a target. The
.B sh
mood does not accept \-R.
.TP
.BI \-o " option"
The
.B set \-o
option is on. [[ ]] accepts \-o in every mood, and test only in the Bash moods.
.TP
.IB file1 " \-ef " file2
The two files are the same file.
.TP
.IB file1 " \-nt " file2 ", " file1 " \-ot " file2
file1 is newer or older than file2. Both files must exist.
.TP
.IB s1 " == " s2 ", " s1 " = " s2 ", " s1 " != " s2
The strings are equal or differ. In [[ ]] the right side is a pattern unless it
is quoted. test accepts == only in the Bash moods.
.TP
.IB s1 " < " s2 ", " s1 " > " s2
s1 sorts before or after s2.
.TP
.IB s " =~ " regex
In [[ ]] only, s matches the extended regular expression. A quoted part of the
regex is literal. BASH_REMATCH receives the match and its groups, an unmatched
group is empty, and a dot matches a newline. An invalid regex is an error with
status 1, where Bash returns 2.
.TP
.IB n1 " \-eq " n2 ", " \-ne ", " \-lt ", " \-le ", " \-gt ", " \-ge
Integer comparisons. In [[ ]] each operand is an arithmetic expression. In test
each operand must be a decimal integer, which may have blanks around it.
.PP
.BI ! " expr"
negates, ( ) groups, and && and || combine inside [[ ]]. test uses \-a and \-o
for and and or, and its outcome depends on the number of arguments, as POSIX
specifies. A malformed [[ ]] fails with status 2.
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
After a simple command is expanded, the shell looks for the command word in
this order: functions, enabled builtins, cached program paths, PATH, and, when
the mood, the options, or
.B enable
allow them, bundled utilities. A word holding a slash runs that path directly.
Running a command or
.B hash
caches its path. A cached path stays until PATH is assigned,
.B hash \-r
runs, or a directory change affects a relative PATH entry.
.PP
A program runs in a separate process with the exported variables as its
environment. A command that is not found returns 127, and a file that cannot be
run returns 126. A command path ending in a directory separator fails with
status 126 when the target is not a directory. A file without a recognized
executable format runs as a shell script in a new shell, without the variables,
functions, and traps of the caller.
.PP
In an interactive shell, a single directory path in command position changes
to that directory as
.B cd
would, unless a function, builtin, program, or bundled utility of that name
exists. A word of only dots moves up one directory per dot after the first.
The
.B autocd
option controls this and is on in every mood, and scripts are never affected.
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT
The shell has an execution environment that consists of the open files, the
working directory, the file creation mask, the resource limits, the traps, the
variables and their attributes, the functions, the options, the aliases, the
directory stack, and the job table.
.PP
A subshell, a command substitution, a pipeline stage that runs in its own
process, and an asynchronous list receive a copy of this environment, and
their changes do not reach the shell. Traps that the shell catches are reset to
their defaults in them, and traps that the shell ignores stay ignored. A
subshell cannot wait for a job of its parent.
.PP
A program receives the open descriptors, the working directory, the creation
mask, the resource limits, the ignored signals, and the exported variables.
Every descriptor the shell opens for itself is closed in the program.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
At startup the shell imports the variables of its environment and marks them
for export. A variable inherited this way, and one marked with
.B export
or
.BR "declare \-x" ,
is passed to every program the shell starts. A prefix assignment adds a
variable for one command. The values that the shell maintains, such as
SECONDS, IFS, SHELLOPTS, and the KOSH variables, are not taken from the
environment.
.PP
On Windows variable names are not case-sensitive, and a value holds at most
32,766 UTF-16 units, so a child receives a longer value truncated. Variables
whose names begin with KOSH_INTERNAL_ are reserved.
.SH SHELL ERRORS
A syntax error in a script, a
.B \-c
string, an eval, or a sourced file keeps all of it from running, and the
status is 2. The parser reports every syntax error in one run. In the
.B kosh
mood, analysis can also reject a script before it runs, with status 1, as
described under
.BR DIAGNOSTICS .
An interactive shell rejects the line and reads the next one.
.PP
In the
.B kosh
mood every arithmetic and expansion error ends the script with status 1. A
usage error of a builtin, such as an unknown option, ends the script with the
status of the error, except for declare, trap, getopts, compopt, and printf.
With valid usage, a failure of bg, cd, dirs, fg, kill, let, local, mapfile,
popd, pushd, read, set, shift, test, ulimit, umask, or z also ends the
script, and a failure of another builtin, such as a missing alias, only sets
its status. A failed redirection of a special builtin or a compound command
ends the script with status 1, and a write error on standard output, an
invalid regular expression, and a malformed [[ ]] also end it.
.PP
In the
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods an arithmetic error in $(( )), a subscript, a substring offset, or an
assignment to an integer variable abandons the rest of the input line, as in
Bash. The remaining commands on the line do not run, $? is 1 on the next line,
and neither
.B set \-e
nor an ERR trap acts on it. An eval, a sourced file, a subshell, and a command
substitution count as separate lines. An error in (( )),
.BR let ,
a [[ ]] operand, or a C-style for header fails only that command with status
1. Outside a here-document body, a $(( )) error ends a non-interactive shell in
the
.B bash-posix
mood, with status 127 for a
.B \-c
string and 1 for a script, and in the
.B sh
mood with status 2. An interactive shell abandons the line.
.PP
Other expansion errors follow Bash 5.3. A glob matching nothing under failglob
abandons the line in the
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods. A bad substitution, such as ${x!y} or ${x:}, and an assignment to a
read-only variable abandon the line in the
.B bash
mood and end a non-interactive shell in the
.B bash-posix
and
.B sh
moods. An unknown transformation such as ${v@Z}, an unset parameter under
.BR "set \-u" ,
and ${name?word} end the script in every mood.
.PP
In the
.B sh
and
.B bash-posix
moods, a syntax error in an eval string or a sourced file ends a
non-interactive shell with status 2, and the other moods fail only that
command. In the
.B sh
mood, an error of a regular builtin, such as a missing cd directory or an
invalid test operand, fails that command with status 2 and the script goes on.
An error of a special builtin or of local, such as shift past the last
parameter or export of an invalid identifier, ends a non-interactive shell with
status 2 unless
.B command
runs it. A failed exec ends a non-interactive shell in every mood.
.PP
An error that ends a non-interactive shell passes through an eval and a
sourced file, as in Bash and dash. A subshell, a command substitution, a trap
action, and a startup file contain it, and the command that started them
receives the status.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The status of a command is a number from 0 to 255. Zero means success and any
other value means failure.
.TP
.B 0
Success, and the status of an empty command, of a compound command that ran no
list, and of an asynchronous command.
.TP
.B 1
A general failure, such as a false condition, a failed builtin, or analysis
that rejected a script.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error of a builtin or the shell, or a syntax error.
.TP
.B 126
A command that was found but cannot be run, such as a directory, a file without
execute permission, or a path ending in a separator that is not a directory.
.TP
.B 127
A command that was not found, or a script operand that cannot be opened.
.TP
.BR 128 + \fIn\fR
A command ended by signal n, such as 130 for SIGINT, 141 for SIGPIPE, and 143
for SIGTERM.
.PP
The shell exits with the status of the last command it ran, or with the
operand of
.BR exit ,
taken modulo 256. When an expansion error ends a non-interactive shell, the
status is 1 in the
.B kosh
mood and 2 in the
.B sh
mood. In the Bash moods an unset parameter under nounset, ${name:?}, and a bad
substitution that ends the shell return 127 from a
.B \-c
string, as in Bash, and 1 from a script.
.PP
.BR \-\-lint ,
.BR \-\-format ,
and
.B \-\-apply
return 0 when every input succeeds, 1 when an input cannot be read, parsed,
formatted, or replaced or analysis reports an error, and 2 for an invalid
option combination. Warning-only findings return 0.
.SH SIGNALS
The shell ignores SIGPIPE, and a program it starts receives the default, so a
producer killed by SIGPIPE returns status 141. An interactive shell also
ignores SIGTERM, SIGQUIT, SIGTSTP, and SIGTTIN. SIGINT interrupts the running
command or the line being edited and reports
.BR Interrupted ,
with status 130. A shell that does not trap SIGHUP, and a non-interactive shell
that does not trap SIGTERM or SIGQUIT, is terminated by the signal, so its
parent sees status 128 plus the signal number.
.PP
When a signal ends a command, the shell prints a line such as
.B [Process 2585: Killed, signal 9]
on standard error, once for each killed pipeline stage, and the status is 128
plus the signal number. SIGPIPE and SIGINT print no such line.
.PP
.B trap
sets the action of a signal or of the conditions EXIT, ERR, DEBUG, and RETURN.
A trapped signal runs its action at the next command boundary. The CHLD trap
runs once for every child the shell reaps, and under
.B set \-m
a job that stops counts too. When a trapped signal arrives while
.B wait
blocks,
.B wait
returns 128 plus the signal number and the job keeps running. On Windows a
background process runs in its own console process group.
.PP
As process 1, the shell reaps orphaned children it did not start, before each
command and while it waits. On SIGTERM it sends SIGTERM and then SIGCONT to
every other process, interrupts the running command, waits for every remaining
child, and exits with status 143.
.SH JOB CONTROL
A job is a pipeline or list that the shell runs in the background, or a
foreground pipeline that was stopped. Every stage of an asynchronous pipeline
belongs to one job, and POSIX stages share a process group. The final stage
determines $!, the status, and the job identity, except that under pipefail the
rightmost failing stage may determine the status. A stopped stage is recorded
even when it is not the last, and
.B fg
and
.B bg
resume every stage. Job control is on in an interactive shell, where Ctrl\-Z
stops the foreground job.
.PP
A job is referred to by a job spec. %\fIn\fR is job n, %+, %%, and % are the
current job, and %\- is the previous one. %\fIstring\fR is the job whose command
begins with string, and %?\fIstring\fR the job whose command contains it. When
several jobs match, the lowest-numbered one is used. A bare number is also
accepted as a job number. In the
.B kosh
mood an unquoted %?string is a glob, so it needs quotes.
.PP
.B jobs
and job notifications print one line per job, as in Bash. The line holds the
job number with a + for the current job or a \- for the previous one, the
process with
.BR "jobs \-l" ,
the state, and the command. A job ended by a signal shows its description,
such as Terminated, and an exited job shows Done, or Exit and its status. An
interactive shell reports a job that finished or changed state before the next
prompt.
.PP
A job reported by
.BR wait ,
.BR jobs ,
or a notification leaves the job table, and a later
.B wait
on its process returns the remembered status. The shell remembers the 1024
most recent statuses, and a
.B wait
without operands discards them. A subshell, a pipeline stage, an asynchronous
command, and a coprocess cannot wait for a job of their parent.
.PP
The shell never sends SIGHUP to its jobs, so running background jobs continue
after it exits, and it exits even while jobs are stopped.
.B disown
removes a job from the table.
.SH PROMPTING
An interactive shell prints PS1 before it reads a command. PS0 is printed after
a command is read and before it runs. PROMPT_COMMAND runs before each prompt.
It is a command or, as an indexed array, a list of commands, which run in
order. Each sees the status and $_ of the previous command, and the hook does
not change them. PS2 is "> " by default, but the editor submits the whole
buffer on Enter with no continuation prompt. PS3 is the prompt of
.BR select .
PS4 is printed before each line of
.B xtrace
output, "+ " by default, and its first byte repeats once per enclosing
substitution, eval, source, and trap action.
.PP
PS0, PS1, PS2, and the right prompt undergo parameter expansion, command
substitution, and arithmetic expansion each time they are printed, after the
backslash escapes below are replaced. PS4 is printed as written. The default
PS1 shows the git branch with ahead and behind counts, the user, host, and
directory, and a bullet.
.PP
RPS1, or RPROMPT when RPS1 is unset or empty, is a right prompt drawn on the
first input row. It is expanded like PS1, blanks at either end are dropped,
and it is hidden when there is no room or when it holds a control character
other than a color sequence. An interactive shell started with neither set
uses a dim "\eL \eM". A nonempty KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1 replaces PS1 on a line that
Enter submits or Ctrl\-C interrupts, so KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1='\e$ ' leaves "$ "
before each command in the scrollback. KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1 does the same for
the right prompt. An interactive shell started without it sets it to \e[\e],
which erases the right prompt, and KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1='\eL' keeps each
duration.
.PP
The prompts recognize these escapes.
.TP
.BR \eu ", " \eh ", " \eH
The user, the host up to the first dot, and the full host.
.TP
.BR \ek ", " \eK
\eh and \eH, colored on a color terminal.
.TP
.BR \ew ", " \eW ", " \eP
The working directory with HOME shown as ~, its last component, and the \ew
form shortened to ... and its last 21 bytes when it is longer than 24 bytes.
.TP
.B \eg
The git branch, as in KOSH_GIT_BRANCH.
.TP
.BR \e$ ", " \e?
# for the superuser and $ otherwise, and the status of the last command.
.TP
.B \ej
The number of jobs.
.TP
.BR \eL ", " \eM
The duration of the last command, empty under 5 milliseconds, and the resident
memory it used. \eM is the highest peak of its processes and their
descendants, or the growth of the shell during the command if larger, and it
is empty when the shell only reused memory.
.TP
.B \e.
A bullet colored by the last status. It is white after 0, bold red after 1,
and on a truecolor terminal it shifts toward magenta in four steps for higher
statuses.
.TP
.BR \et ", " \eT ", " \e@ ", " \eA ", " \ed
The time as HH:MM:SS in 24-hour and 12-hour form, the 12-hour time as HH:MM
with AM or PM, the time as HH:MM, and the date as weekday, month, and day.
.TP
.BI \eD{ format }
The time formatted by strftime, or as HH:MM:SS for an empty format.
.TP
.BR \es ", " \ev ", " \eV
The basename of the invocation path without a login dash or .exe, the major
and minor version, and the full version.
.TP
.BR \e! ", " \e#
The history number of the next command, and the command number, counting from
1 for each line read from a terminal, script, or standard input.
.TP
.B \el
The last component of the terminal of standard input, or tty.
.TP
.BR \en ", " \er ", " \ee ", " \ea ", " \e\e
A newline, a carriage return, an escape, a bell, and a backslash.
.TP
.BI \e nnn
The byte with octal value nnn, of one to three digits.
.TP
.BR \e[ ", " \e]
Begin and end a region that takes no space on the screen, such as a color
sequence. A raw 001 or 002 byte in PS0, PS1, RPS1, KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1, or
KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1 does the same.
.PP
Any other backslash sequence is printed as written. Text inserted by an escape
is never expanded, and the user, host, directory, branch, shell, and version
draw a control byte in caret notation and a byte that is not UTF-8 as \exHH.
The variables KOSH_PS1_USER (\eu), KOSH_PS1_HOST (\eh), KOSH_PS1_HOST_FULL
(\eH), KOSH_PS1_CWD (\ew), KOSH_PS1_CWD_BASE (\eW), KOSH_PS1_CWD_SHORT (\eP),
KOSH_PS1_GIT_BRANCH (\eg), KOSH_PS1_PROMPT_SIGN (\e$), KOSH_PS1_STATUS (\e?),
KOSH_PS1_JOBS (\ej), KOSH_PS1_DURATION (\eL), KOSH_PS1_MEMORY (\eM),
KOSH_PS1_DOT (\e.), KOSH_PS1_TIME (\et), KOSH_PS1_TIME_12 (\eT),
KOSH_PS1_TIME_AMPM (\e@), KOSH_PS1_TIME_SHORT (\eA), KOSH_PS1_DATE (\ed),
KOSH_PS1_SHELL (\es), KOSH_PS1_VERSION (\ev), KOSH_PS1_VERSION_FULL (\eV),
KOSH_PS1_TTY (\el), KOSH_PS1_HISTORY_NUMBER (\e!), and
KOSH_PS1_COMMAND_NUMBER (\e#) hold the current text of each escape, and
assignments to them are ignored.
.PP
The terminal title shows the user and directory while the shell waits, and the
command while a foreground command runs. Output of PROMPT_COMMAND or PS1 can
replace the idle title.
.SH LINE EDITING
The interactive editor starts in emacs mode. Use
.B set \-o vi
to select vi mode and
.B set \-o emacs
to return. The prompts are described under
.BR PROMPTING .
Input taller than the terminal shows the rows around the caret and
leaves two rows for hints, without the ghost suggestion and right prompt. A
control character is drawn in caret notation, such as ^[ for ESC, and a
filename holding one completes in the $'...' form. Bracketed paste inserts text
without running it.
.PP
Kill commands save the erased text in a 16-entry kill ring for the session,
and consecutive kills join into one entry. Ctrl\-K on an empty line joins the
next line, and on an empty last line removes it. Undo reverts a yank or a
last-word insertion with all its replacements in one step. Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E, or
.B :e
in vi mode, opens the line in VISUAL, EDITOR, or vi; when the editor exits with
status 0 the saved text replaces the line without running it, and otherwise
the line is unchanged and a message appears.
.PP
Ctrl\-Z undoes while the editor collects a line, and stops a foreground program
otherwise. Ctrl\-Shift\-Z redoes when the terminal reports it apart from
Ctrl\-Z; the editor requests such reports through the kitty keyboard protocol
and xterm modifyOtherKeys, which tmux passes on with its
.B extended\-keys
option on.
.B koshconf set editor.request_extended_key_reports off
stops the requests. On the Windows console both keys undo and Ctrl\-^ redoes.
.PP
On a non-empty line, Up recalls only history entries that begin with the text
typed before the first Up, and Down moves back; an empty line steps through all
history. editor.history.arrow_keys_search_by_typed_prefix, on by default,
controls this.
.B koshconf set editor.auto_close_brackets_and_quotes on
makes a typed (, [, {, or quote insert its closer, step over a typed closer,
and delete an empty pair on Backspace.
.PP
When the caret is on or just after a bracket, the bracket and its partner are
highlighted; a bracket inside quotes, a comment, a here-document, or escaped
never pairs.
.SS Hints
Once the caret passes the command word, hints appear below the input under a
.B synopsis
heading. Up to 16 rows explain the command, its subcommand, and its flags.
The current word is marked with
.BR > .
Hints come from manual pages, builtin or koshkit help, alias definitions,
and function locations. After a key prefix such as Ctrl\-X or a vi operator,
the hints list the keys that complete it. Hints are hidden while a menu or
search is open.
.PP
After a short pause, the hint of an external program loads its section 1
manual page, a subcommand page such as
.BR git\-commit (1),
or, for an allowlisted command, its help output.
.BR man ,
.BR manpath ,
and the program run only from an absolute directory owned by the user or root
that no group or other user can write, and never through an empty or relative
PATH element. A keystroke never runs the command itself. A Tab that needs a
load shows
.B loading...
while it waits.
.PP
After the same pause, the first diagnostic for the current input appears as
.BR error ,
.BR warning ,
or
.BR note .
This check does not follow sourced files, look up commands, or open files.
If the input cannot be parsed, its first error appears under
.BR "syntax error" .
Enter submits the whole buffer without a continuation prompt. An unfinished
construct is therefore reported as an error.
.PP
Set editor.hints.show_command_synopsis to off to hide command explanations
and stop their documentation loads. Set editor.hints.show_live_diagnostics
to off to hide findings and syntax errors. Both options are on by default.
Turning off both also hides the pending-key rows.
.SS Completion
Ghost text offers prefix matches from completion and history; the right arrow
or End at the end of the line accepts it, and Ctrl\-Right or Alt\-F its next
word. Tab also offers smart-case and subsequence matches. An empty command
position offers keywords, builtins, functions, aliases, bundled utilities, and
PATH programs. Other words complete as paths, a word beginning with $ or ${ as
a variable, a word beginning with ~ as a user, and a word beginning with a dash
as a flag of the command. A spec registered with
.B complete
takes precedence for its command. A directory candidate ends in a slash. The
operand of
.B cd
or
.B pushd
completes directories, also under each CDPATH entry unless it is absolute or
starts with a dot or tilde. Targets of
.B make
come from the Makefile, and those after
.B cmake \-\-build
.I dir
.B \-\-target
from
.IR dir /CMakeFiles/TargetDirectories.txt,
without running either program or any $(shell ...) function.
.PP
An explicit Tab first inserts the longest common prefix. A sole candidate
replaces the token, followed by a space as
completion.add_space_after_completed_word selects:
.B off
(the default),
.BR on ,
or
.BR on\-excluding\-trailing\-slash .
Several candidates go to the selector of
.B \-\-tab\-selector
or completion.menu_style.
.PP
The interactive selector draws a menu below the line, headed
.B selecting completions
or, for Ctrl\-R,
.BR "incremental history search" .
Typing narrows the menu: command, path, and koshconf option lists keep the
best of the exact-prefix, smart-case-prefix, and subsequence tiers, and other
lists keep every case-insensitive prefix match. A history entry matches when
it begins with the query, contains the query, or contains the query's bytes
in order. Backspace widens the list. Typing a blank or slash, or erasing past
the start of the token, refreshes the candidates after a pause. Accepting a
directory opens a menu of its entries.
.PP
The external selector passes the candidates to
.B KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND
and replaces the token with its output. Dismissing the selector leaves the
line unchanged. A single candidate or a session without a controlling
terminal uses the plain listing. An empty selector command, a command that
cannot be found, or an exit status other than 0, 1, or 130 also uses the plain
listing after a message.
.PP
The plain selector prints the candidates as a column list, which a terminal
without decoration support always receives; for Ctrl\-R it keeps the
incremental search inside the editor.
.PP
Ctrl\-R searches the history through the selected selector, newest first, with
the line as the query; an accepted entry replaces the line. The search uses a
snapshot of the shared history file and does not change the private history.
.SS Terminal integration
An interactive shell with terminal output writes OSC 133 A, B, C, and D marks
and OSC 7 working directory marks, plus OSC 633 marks when TERM_PROGRAM is
vscode, so terminals can find prompts, commands, statuses, and the directory.
No mark is written when TERM is dumb or
.B KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION
is 0.
.SS Emacs mode keys
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-A ", " Home
Move to the start of the line.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-E ", " End
Move to the end of the line, or accept the ghost suggestion at the end of the
buffer.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-Home ", " Ctrl\-End ", " Ctrl\-Up ", " Ctrl\-Down ", " Ctrl\-PageUp
Move to the start or end of the buffer. Ctrl\-PageUp moves to the start.
.TP
.BR PageUp ", " PageDown
Move the cursor up or down by the rows of the screen.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-B ", " Left
Move one character left.
.TP
.B Right
Move one character right, or accept the ghost suggestion at the end of the
buffer.
.TP
.BR Alt\-B ", " Ctrl\-Left ", " Alt\-Left
Move to the start of the previous word.
.TP
.BR Alt\-F ", " Ctrl\-Right ", " Alt\-Right
Move past the next word, or accept the next word of the ghost suggestion.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-P ", " Up
Move up a row, or recall an older entry from the first row. A typed or edited
multi-line buffer that joins no line with a backslash needs a second press.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-N ", " Down
Move down a row, or recall a newer entry from the last row.
.TP
.BR Alt\-< ", " Alt\-, ", " Alt\->
Recall the oldest or newest entry, or in
.B slurp
move to the start or end of the file.
.TP
.BR Enter ", " Ctrl\-J ", " Ctrl\-M ", " Shift\-Enter
Run the line, or continue it after a trailing backslash. A typed or edited
multi-line buffer that joins no line with a backslash runs on a second press.
.TP
.B Alt\-Enter
Insert a line break.
.TP
.BR Tab ", " Shift\-Tab ", " Ctrl\-Space
Complete the token. Ctrl\-Space does so only on POSIX.
.TP
.BR Backspace ", " Ctrl\-H ", " Delete
Delete the character before or under the cursor.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-D
Delete the character under the cursor, or exit on an empty line.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-W ", " Alt\-Backspace ", " Alt\-H
Kill the previous word.
.TP
.BR Alt\-D ", " Ctrl\-Delete
Kill to the next word boundary, which is the rest of the word under the cursor,
or the blanks before the next word.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-K ", " Ctrl\-U
Kill to the end or start of the line.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-Y ", " Alt\-Y
Yank the newest kill, or replace the yank with the next older kill.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-T ", " Alt\-T
Swap the characters or words around the cursor.
.TP
.BR Alt\-U ", " Alt\-L ", " Alt\-C
Uppercase, lowercase, or capitalize to the end of the word.
.TP
.B Alt\-\e
Delete blanks around the cursor.
.TP
.BR Alt\-. ", " Alt\-_
Insert the last word of an older history entry on each press.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-_ ", " Ctrl\-Z ", " "Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-U"
Undo.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-^ ", " Ctrl\-Shift\-Z
Redo.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E
Edit the line in an external editor.
.TP
.BR Alt\-X ", " "Ctrl\-X z"
Open the M\-x minibuffer, or repeat the last M\-x command.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-C
Exit with the status of the last command.
.TP
.BR "Ctrl\-X s" ", " "Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-S"
Save the file in
.BR slurp .
.TP
.B Ctrl\-S
Find text in the buffer as it is typed, forward and wrapping around; text
without capitals matches either case. Ctrl\-S and Ctrl\-R move to the next and
previous match, and on empty text reuse the last search. Enter keeps the
cursor there, and Escape returns it.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-R ", " Ctrl\-F
Search the history. In
.BR slurp ,
Ctrl\-R finds text backward.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-L
Clear the screen.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-C
Abandon the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-V
Enter multi-cursor mode.
.PP
Shift with Left, Right, Up, Down, Home, End, PageUp, or PageDown selects from
the caret to where the key moves it, and a Shift move that cannot move the
caret, such as Shift\-Up on a single row, acts as the key alone. While text is
selected, Backspace and Delete erase it, typed or pasted text replaces it,
Ctrl\-W kills it, Alt\-W copies it, and Ctrl\-V enters multi-cursor mode with
it. Ctrl\-G, Escape, or a move without Shift leaves the selection, and any
other key leaves it and acts. Alt with Up, Down, Left, Right, Home, or End acts
as the key alone, except where an entry above gives it a meaning, and
Ctrl\-Backspace deletes one character.
.SS Vi insert mode keys
Every emacs key works except Ctrl\-V, Alt\-X, and the Ctrl\-X chords. Arrow
keys recall history.
.TP
.B Escape
Enter command mode and move one character left.
.SS Vi command mode keys
In command mode the arrow keys move between rows without recalling history.
.TP
.BR 1 \- 9 ", then " 0 \- 9
Count for the next command.
.TP
.BI \(dq x
Use register
.I x
(a to z, either case) for the next delete, change, yank, or paste.
.TP
.BR h ", " l ", " Left ", " Right ", " Backspace ", " Space ", " Ctrl\-B ", " Ctrl\-F
Move left or right.
.TP
.BR w ", " W ", " b ", " B ", " e ", " E
Move to the next start, previous start, or end of a word or blank-separated
word.
.TP
.BR 0 ", " ^ ", " $ ", " Home ", " End ", " Ctrl\-A ", " Ctrl\-E
Move to the line start, first non-blank, or line end.
.TP
.BI f x ", " F x ", " t x ", " T x ", " ; ", " ,
Find
.I x
forward or backward, stop before it, or repeat the find.
.TP
.BR k ", " j ", " Up ", " Down ", " Ctrl\-P ", " Ctrl\-N
Move between rows; k and j recall history from the first or last row.
.TP
.BR gg ", " G
Move to the first or last row, or row N with a count.
.TP
.BR d ", " c ", " y
Delete, change, or yank over a motion; doubled, or with j, k, Up, or Down,
over whole lines.
.TP
.BR gq ", " gqq
Wrap the lines of a motion, or the current line, at editor.wrap_width (80),
keeping the indentation of each paragraph's first line.
.TP
.BR x ", " X ", " D ", " C ", " s ", " S
Delete under or before the cursor, delete or change to the line end, change one
character, or change the line.
.TP
.BI r x ", " ~
Replace the character, or toggle its case and move right.
.TP
.BR p ", " P
Paste after or before the cursor.
.TP
.BR o ", " O ", " J
Open a line below or above, or join the next line with one space.
.TP
.BR { ", " }
Move to the previous or next empty line.
.TP
.BR i ", " a ", " I ", " A ", " R
Insert before or after the cursor, at the first non-blank, or at the line end;
R inserts without overwriting.
.TP
.B .
Repeat the last change other than a yank.
.TP
.BR u ", " Ctrl\-_ ", " Ctrl\-Z
Undo.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-R ", " Ctrl\-^ ", " Ctrl\-Shift\-Z
Redo.
.TP
.BR / ", " ? ", " n ", " N
Search the history, or in
.B slurp
find text forward or backward and repeat the find in either direction.
.TP
.B :
Open the colon command line.
.TP
.BR v ", " V ", " Ctrl\-V
Select characters, lines, or a rectangle.
.TP
.BR Enter ", " Ctrl\-D ", " Ctrl\-C ", " Ctrl\-L
Act as in emacs mode.
.SS Vi visual and rectangle keys
Motions move the end of the selection, or the rectangle corner, without a
count. Visual mode accepts
.BR d ,
.B x
(delete),
.B c
(change),
.B y
(yank),
.B gq
(wrap, repeatable with .), and
.B :
(colon commands on the selected lines, shown as :'<,'>). Rectangle mode
accepts
.BR d ,
.B x
(delete), and
.BR I ,
.BR c ,
.B C
(insert at the left column of every line when insert mode ends). Escape,
.B v
or Ctrl\-V, and any other unrecognized key leave the selection, and Enter,
Ctrl\-D, and Ctrl\-C leave it and act as in emacs mode.
.SS Vi colon commands
Enter runs the command, and Escape, Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-D, or Backspace on an empty
line closes it. Unknown commands do nothing.
.TP
.BR :q ", " :q! ", " :quit ", " :wq ", " :wq! ", " :wqa ", " :wqa! ", " :x
Exit with the status of the last command. In
.BR slurp ,
the w forms save first.
.TP
.BI : N ", " :$
Move to row N of the buffer, or to the last row.
.TP
.BR :w ", " :write
Save the file in
.BR slurp .
.TP
.BI :s/ old / new / \fR[\fBg\fR]
Replace the first match of the basic regular expression
.I old
on the caret row, or every match with
.BR g ;
.B :%s
acts on every row. Any punctuation can delimit, and in
.I new
.B &
is the match and
.BR \e1 \- \e9
its groups.
.BR :& ,
.BR :&& ,
and
.B :s
repeat the last substitution,
.B :&&
with its
.B g
flag, and
.B :%&
and
.B :%&&
on every row.
.TP
.BI :set " option" \fR[\fB=\fIvalue\fR|\fB?\fR], " " :set " no" option
Set, show, or turn off a
.B koshconf
option; Tab completes it.
.TP
.BR :save " [\fIoption\fR]"
Write the option, or every option, to
.IR kosh.conf .
.TP
.BR :format ", " :%format
Format the caret row or the buffer as
.B \-\-format
does; a source that does not parse is kept and its error shown.
.TP
.BR :e ", " :edit ", " :u ", " :undo ", " :red ", " :redo
Edit in an external editor, undo, or redo.
.TP
.BR :h ", " :help
List the colon commands until the next key.
.SS Emacs M\-x commands
Tab completes a command, Enter runs it, and Escape, Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-D, or
Backspace on an empty minibuffer closes it. The hint rows list the matching
commands, each with what it does when six or fewer match. The commands are
.B help
(list the commands),
.B replace\-string
and
.B replace\-regexp
(replace every match in the buffer, with \e& and \e1\-\e9 in a regexp
replacement),
.B repeat
(as Ctrl\-X z),
.BR set\-option ,
.BR get\-option ,
and
.B save\-option
(as :set and :save),
.B format
and
.B format\-buffer
(as :format and :%format),
.B fill\-paragraph
(as gq on the paragraph),
.B isearch\-forward
and
.BR isearch\-backward ,
.B kill\-kosh
(as Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-C),
.B undo
and
.BR undo\-redo ,
and the commands of the matching keys:
.BR beginning\-of\-line ,
.BR end\-of\-line ,
.BR forward\-word ,
.BR backward\-word ,
.BR scroll\-down\-command ,
.BR scroll\-up\-command ,
.BR beginning\-of\-history ,
.BR end\-of\-history ,
.BR delete\-char ,
.BR backward\-delete\-char ,
.BR kill\-word ,
.BR backward\-kill\-word ,
.BR kill\-line ,
.BR unix\-line\-discard ,
.BR yank ,
.BR yank\-last\-arg ,
.BR transpose\-chars ,
.BR transpose\-words ,
.BR upcase\-word ,
.BR downcase\-word ,
.BR capitalize\-word ,
.BR delete\-horizontal\-space ,
and
.BR clear\-screen .
.SS Multi-cursor keys
Up, Down, Ctrl\-P, and Ctrl\-N add a cursor on the row above or below. When
the mode starts from a selection, Up and Down add a new row holding a copy of
the selected text above the top row or below the bottom row, with a cursor on
it.
Left, Right, Home, End, Ctrl\-B, Ctrl\-F, Ctrl\-A, and Ctrl\-E move every
cursor, and typed characters and Backspace act at every cursor. Ctrl\-G,
Escape, and any other unrecognized key leave the mode, and Enter, Ctrl\-D, and
Ctrl\-C leave it and act as in emacs mode.
.SS Completion menu keys
.TP
.BR Down ", " Ctrl\-N ", " Up ", " Ctrl\-P ", " Shift\-Tab
Highlight the next or previous row, wrapping around.
.TP
.B Tab
Accept the row, opening a directory.
.TP
.B Right
Accept the row and offer the completions that follow it.
.TP
.B Enter
Close the menu and run the line as it stands.
.TP
.BR \fIcharacter\fR ", " Backspace
Narrow or widen the rows.
.TP
.B Escape
Close the menu and keep the edited line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-G
Close the menu and restore the original line, as does any other unrecognized
key.
.TP
.I other
Close the menu, then act on the line.
.SS History search keys
In the interactive selector, Enter and Tab accept the entry without running
it, Escape, Ctrl\-G, and other unrecognized keys restore the line, and the
other keys act as in the completion menu. The plain selector accepts:
.TP
.BR Down ", " Ctrl\-N ", " Ctrl\-R
Step to an older match.
.TP
.BR Up ", " Ctrl\-P ", " Ctrl\-F ", " Shift\-Tab
Step to a newer match.
.TP
.BR \fIcharacter\fR ", " Backspace
Extend or shorten the query.
.TP
.BR Enter ", " Tab
Accept the match without running it.
.TP
.BR Escape ", " Ctrl\-G
Cancel and restore the line, as does any other unrecognized key.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-C ", " Ctrl\-D
Cancel, restore the line, and act on it.
.TP
.I other
Accept the match, then act on the line.
.SH HISTORY
An interactive shell records each submitted command line as one event, even
when it spans several lines, while the
.B history
option is on, which is the default. A non-interactive shell records no events,
whatever the option says. A line that
repeats the previous event is not recorded again. A line with a history
expansion error is not recorded.
.PP
The history file is KOSH_HISTORY_FILE, ~/.kosh_history by default. An
interactive shell loads it at startup into its own list and appends each
command to the file as soon as it is submitted, so other running shells can
read it. It does not import the commands that other shells append later.
Ctrl\-R searches a snapshot of the whole file. A non-interactive shell writes
the file only through
.B history \-w
or
.BR "history \-a" .
.PP
The file holds one event per line, with a newline in an event written as \en
and a backslash as \e\e. It has mode 0600, and the shell locks its directory
while it writes. A record over 65535 bytes is skipped when the file is read.
KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE is the number of events kept in the list and in the file,
4096 by default, and 0 disables history. When the shell exits, it rewrites the
file with only the newest KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE events.
.PP
editor.history.file_path and editor.history.max_entries set the two variables
from the settings files, and a value inherited from the environment takes
precedence. The variables are exported only when they were inherited exported
or are exported explicitly. The Bash history variables have no effect, as
PARAMETERS lists them.
.B history
and
.B fc
list, edit, and run events.
.SH HISTORY EXPANSION
In an interactive shell, while the
.B histexpand
option is on, which is the default, an unquoted ! in a line starts a history
reference. The shell replaces the reference before it parses the line. A
non-interactive shell does not expand history. A reference has an event, an
optional word designator, and optional modifiers. The line with its references
replaced is printed before it runs.
History expansion takes place inside double quotes, and a single-quoted or
backslash-escaped ! stays literal.
.SS Event designators
.TP
.B !!
The previous event.
.TP
.BI ! n
Event number n.
.TP
.BI !\- n
The event n before the current one.
.TP
.BI ! string
The newest event that begins with string.
.TP
.BI !? string ?
The newest event that contains string. The closing ? may be omitted at the end
of the line.
.TP
.B !#
The current line up to this reference. It takes no word designators or
modifiers.
.TP
.BI ^ old ^ new ^
At the start of a line, the previous event with the first old replaced by new,
as !!:s/old/new/ does.
.SS Word designators
A colon separates a word designator from the event, and it may be omitted
before ^, $, and *. Words are counted from 0, the command.
.TP
.BR :^ ", " :$ ", " :*
The first argument, the last argument, and every argument.
.TP
.BI : n
Word n.
.TP
.BI : n \- m ", " :\- m ", " : n * ", " : n \-$
Words n through m, words 0 through m, words n through the last, and words n
through the last.
.TP
.BI : n \-
Words n through the next to last.
.PP
!$, !^, and !* are the last argument, the first argument, and every argument of
the previous event.
.SS Modifiers
Each modifier follows a colon.
.TP
.BR :h ", " :t ", " :r ", " :e
Remove the last path component, keep only the last component, remove a
trailing suffix, or keep only the suffix.
.TP
.BI :s/ old / new /
Replace the first occurrence of old, as literal text. Another delimiter may
replace the slash, and the last delimiter may be omitted at the end of the
line. An & in new stands for old.
.TP
.BR :gs ", " :as
Replace every occurrence.
.TP
.B :Gs
Replace the first occurrence in each word.
.TP
.B :&
Repeat the last substitution.
.TP
.BR :q ", " :x
Quote the text as one argument, or each word separately.
.TP
.B :p
Print the result and record it in the history without running it.
.PP
A reference that matches no event, a substitution that finds no match, a
missing substitution text, and an unknown designator are errors, and the
command neither runs nor enters the history.
.SH BUILTINS
A builtin runs inside the shell. The special builtins are :, ., break,
continue, eval, exec, exit, export, readonly, return, set, shift, times, trap,
and unset, and in the Bash moods source is also special. Assignments before a
special builtin remain in the shell, as described under
.BR "Simple commands" ,
and their errors follow the rules under
.BR "SHELL ERRORS" .
The Koshka builtins assimilate, bench, koshconf, koshkit, slurp, and z exist
in every mood. The
.B sh
mood hides let and time.
.PP
A builtin given
.B \-\-help
before its other arguments prints its description, synopsis, and options. In
the compatibility moods echo prints \-\-help and test ignores it, and [
\-\-help reports a missing ] in every mood. Unless an entry states otherwise,
a builtin returns 0 on success, 1 on a failure, and 2 for an unknown option
or a wrong number of operands.
.TP
\fB:\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBtrue\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
.PD
Do nothing and return 0. The arguments are expanded and ignored, except that
\-\-help prints the help.
.TP
\fB.\fR [\fB\-\-mood\fR[\fB=\fR]\fIMOOD\fR] \fIfile\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBsource\fR [\fB\-\-mood\fR[\fB=\fR]\fIMOOD\fR] \fIfile\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
.PD
Read and run the commands of file in the shell, and return the status of the
last one, or the operand of a
.B return
in the file. The arguments become the positional parameters while the file
runs, and the
.B sh
mood ignores them. The
.B sourcepath
option is on by default. While it is on, a file without a slash is searched in
PATH and then in the current directory. A missing or unreadable
file returns 1, and in the
.B sh
mood it ends a non-interactive shell with status 2.
.IP
In the
.B kosh
mood, a Bash startup file (.bashrc, .bash_profile, .bash_login,
\&.bash_logout, .profile, or /etc/profile) runs in the
.B bash
mood with its options, and a file whose shebang selects another shell runs in
that mood. The
.B kosh
mood returns when the file ends, and the variables, functions, and aliases it
defined stay. Analysis does not follow such a file, a syntax error in it fails
the command without ending the shell, and the files it sources keep its mood.
A file without a shebang runs in the current mood.
.B \-\-mood
runs file in MOOD in place of the mood its shebang or name selects, and
restores the previous mood afterwards.
.TP
\fB[\fR \fIexpression\fR \fB]\fR
.PD 0
.TP
\fBtest\fR \fIexpression\fR
.PD
Evaluate a conditional expression, as described under
.BR "CONDITIONAL EXPRESSIONS" ,
and return 0 when it is true and 1 when it is false. No expression is false. A
malformed expression, a missing ], and an operand of an integer comparison that
is not a decimal integer return 2. In the
.B kosh
and
.B sh
moods == is rejected with a hint to use =.
.TP
\fBalias\fR [\fIname\fR[\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR] ...]
Define an alias for each name with a value, and print the definition of each
name without one. Without operands, print every alias as an alias command. An
unknown alias or an empty name returns 1. A name may hold any character except
=.
.TP
\fBassimilate\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fITARGET\fR]
Copy the running executable through scp to TARGET and install it as
.B kosh
in the first usable remote PATH directory, skipping a path that contains sbin.
Without TARGET, install it the same way into the first usable local PATH
directory and create a relative symbolic link to
.B kosh
for each bundled utility and utility alias whose name is free there or already
links to it. On Windows the local form installs kosh.exe and makes a hard link
with the .exe suffix for each utility and link mood, and a later run with a
changed binary makes those links again. A name that another entry in that
directory holds, or that a program elsewhere on PATH provides, stays unlinked,
and each such group is reported in one warning.
.IP
.BI \-\-link\-mood= MOOD
adds a relative bash, dash, sh, or kosh link. It may be repeated and takes
comma-separated values.
.BI \-\-install\-directory= DIR
installs into DIR, even one containing sbin.
.BI \-\-ssh\-command= COMMAND
and
.BI \-\-scp\-command= COMMAND
replace ssh and scp and need TARGET.
.BR \-x ", " \-\-trace
prints progress. The shell asks for confirmation with the target and the full
path of the new binary, as described under
.BR "KOSHKIT UTILITIES" ,
and
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
skips the question. The copy is validated before the installed binary is
replaced, and a failed installation restores the previous file or link.
.TP
\fBbench\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fIcommand\fR ...
Run each command through
.B kosh \-c
and report the wall time, peak resident set, and available performance
counters as a mean, standard deviation, and range. Sampling stops after a
nonzero status, and bench returns 1, unless
.B \-\-ignore\-exit\-code
is given.
.BR \-\-show\-output ,
.BR \-N " (" \-\-no\-shell ),
.BR \-n " (" \-\-runs ),
and
.BR \-t " (" \-\-duration )
control output, direct execution, the sample count, and the time budget in
milliseconds, 3000 by default.
.BR \-w " (" \-\-warmup )
sets the number of unmeasured runs before sampling. Without a value it runs one
to three until two runs agree within 10%, and without the option one
unmeasured run precedes sampling. The run and warmup counts range from 1 to
100000. A note follows the report when a sample is an outlier.
.BR \-p " (" \-\-profile )
is
.B "\-\-runs 1 \-\-extended"
and cannot be combined with
.BR \-n .
A missing command, a count out of range, and other usage errors return 1, and
an interrupt returns 130.
.IP
.BR \-E " (" \-\-extended )
also reports CPU time and usage, waiting time (wall time minus user and system
time, or the average active task count times wall time minus CPU time for a
parallel command), context switches, page faults, block operations, and read
and write calls and bytes. Each platform adds what it can measure. Linux adds
scheduler and hardware counters and, from one extra untimed traced run, system
calls, threads, processes, execs, and task counts. macOS adds processor
counters, run-queue wait, wakeups, energy, and storage figures. Windows adds
whole process-tree counters through a job object. Traced rows are marked
(traced), a row marked * counts only the measured process, and a row the
platform cannot report is omitted.
.TP
\fBbg\fR [\fIjob\fR]
Resume a stopped job in the background, by default the current job. A missing
job returns 1, or 2 in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
\fBbind\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIkeyseq\fB:\fIfunction\fR ...]
Accept the readline options \-lmrvpPsq, \-f, and \-u and their operands
without changing key bindings, and return 0. The key bindings are described
under
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
\fBbreak\fR [\fIn\fR]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBcontinue\fR [\fIn\fR]
.PD
Leave the innermost n enclosing loops, by default 1, or start their next
iteration. A count above the nesting depth applies to the outermost loop. A
count of 0 returns 1 and leaves every enclosing loop in the Bash moods, as Bash
does, and the innermost loop in the
.B kosh
mood. In the
.B sh
mood it returns 2 and ends a non-interactive shell. A count that is not a
number ends a non-interactive shell with status 2 in every mood. Outside a
loop the commands do nothing. A loop in a calling function is not affected.
.TP
\fBbuiltin\fR [\fIname\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBbuiltin \-\-list\fR
.PD
Run the builtin name even when a function of that name exists. A name that is
not a builtin returns 1.
.B \-\-list
prints every builtin, one per line. In the
.B kosh
mood, builtin with no operand lists the builtins under POSIX, BASH, and Koshka
headings.
.TP
\fBcaller\fR [\fIn\fR]
Print the line, function, and file of call frame n, counting from 0 for the
caller of the current function. When n is past the last frame, caller returns
1. Without an operand, caller prints the line and file of the caller inside a
function, a sourced file, or a script file, where the top level prints 0 NULL,
and returns 1 in a command string or standard input. An operand that is not a
whole number returns 2, and a second operand is ignored.
.TP
\fBcd\fR [\fB\-L\fR|\fB\-P\fR] [\fIdir\fR]
Change the working directory to dir, by default HOME, and set PWD and OLDPWD.
An operand of \- changes to OLDPWD and prints it. A relative dir that does not
begin with . or .. is searched in each CDPATH directory, and a directory found
there is printed. A relative directory resolves against the logical PWD, and
\&.. removes its last component, unless
.B \-P
or the
.B physical
option resolves symbolic links first.
.B \-L
keeps the logical path. A missing directory and an unset HOME or OLDPWD return
1, and 2 in the
.B sh
mood, and more than one operand returns 2. In the Bash moods an empty operand
returns 1, and the other moods accept it. A restricted shell refuses cd.
.TP
\fBcommand\fR [\fB\-pVv\fR] \fIname\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Run name as a builtin or program, skipping functions and aliases. A special
builtin run this way loses its special properties.
.B \-p
searches the default PATH.
.B \-v
prints the path, builtin, keyword, function, or alias definition that name
resolves to, and
.B \-V
describes it in words. With \-v or \-V the status is 0 when any operand
resolves, and 1 when none does, or 127 in the
.B sh
mood. Running a command that is not found returns 127, and one that cannot be
run 126.
.TP
\fBcompgen\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIword\fR]
Print the candidates that the options generate and that begin with word, one
per line. The actions \-a (aliases), \-b (builtins), \-c (commands), \-d
(directories), \-e (exported variables), \-f (files), \-g (groups), \-j (jobs),
\-k (reserved words), \-s (services), \-u (users), and \-v (variables), and
.BI "\-A " action
select sources. The actions of \-A are alias, arrayvar, binding, builtin,
command, directory, disabled, enabled, export, file, function, group,
helptopic, hostname, job, keyword, running, service, setopt, shopt, signal,
stopped, user, and variable.
.BI "\-W " wordlist
splits the expanded wordlist,
.BI "\-G " glob
adds pathname matches, and
.BI "\-X " pattern
removes the candidates that match pattern, or keeps only those after a leading
!, and an unescaped & in the pattern stands for the word.
.BI "\-P " prefix
and
.BI "\-S " suffix
add text to each candidate after matching and filtering.
.BI "\-V " array
stores the candidates in an indexed array and prints nothing.
\-o, \-F, and \-C are accepted without effect. No candidate returns 1, and an
unknown action returns 2.
.TP
\fBcomplete\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
Register how the arguments of each command complete. A spec offers the
candidates of its actions, its
.B \-G
glob, its
.B \-W
words, and, on an explicit Tab, the COMPREPLY of its
.B \-F
function and the output lines of its
.B \-C
command, in that order. The
.B \-C
command runs as a command substitution with the command, the word, and the
previous word as arguments and COMP_LINE, COMP_POINT, COMP_KEY, and COMP_TYPE
exported. A completion function receives the same arguments and the COMP_
variables described under
.BR PARAMETERS .
Trailing spaces are removed from each COMPREPLY entry, the spec's \-X, \-P, and
\-S apply, and with the filenames option a slash is appended to each directory.
The ghost suggestion never runs a function or command. The actions are those
of
.BR compgen .
.IP
.B \-o default
and
.B \-o bashdefault
fall back to filenames and to the shell completion when the spec finds
nothing,
.B \-o filenames
treats candidates as filenames,
.B \-o dirnames
adds matching directories when nothing else matched, and
.B \-o plusdirs
adds them always. A spec quotes inserted candidates only when it generates
filenames, as in Bash.
.B \-o fullquote
quotes every candidate,
.B \-o noquote
none,
.B \-o nosort
keeps the generated order, and
.B \-o nospace
adds no space. An empty result falls through to the next completion source. An
unknown
.B \-o
option or
.B \-A
action returns 2 and registers nothing.
.IP
With
.B progcomp_alias
on, a command without a spec uses the spec of the first word of its alias, and
an explicit Tab follows a PATH command to its symlink target. A command still
without a spec uses the
.B \-D
spec, and when its function returns 124 completion restarts with the spec it
registered.
.B \-E
serves an explicit Tab on an empty line and
.B \-I
the command word. Their functions receive
.B _EmptycmD_
and
.B _InitialWorD_
as the command. Of several of
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-E ,
and
.BR \-I ,
the first in that order applies.
.B \-p
prints the specs in Bash order, and
.B \-r
removes the given specs, or all of them. A missing spec returns 1.
.TP
\fBcompopt\fR [\fB\-o\fR|\fB+o\fR \fIoption\fR] [\fB\-DEI\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
.BI "\-o " option
enables a
.B complete \-o
option, and
.BI "+o " option
disables it. If both forms name the same option, it is disabled.
It applies to each given command's spec, or to the
first of the
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-E ,
and
.B \-I
specs, or, inside a completion function with neither, to the current
completion. Without
.B \-o
or
.BR +o ,
it prints one
.B compopt
line per spec. An unknown option returns 2, and a missing spec, or a missing
target outside a completion function, returns 1.
.TP
\fBdeclare\fR [\fB\-aAfFgilnprtux\fR] [\fIname\fR[\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR] ...]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBtypeset\fR [\fB\-aAfFgilnprtux\fR] [\fIname\fR[\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR] ...]
.PD
Set variables and their attributes. Inside a function the variables are local
unless
.B \-g
is given.
.B \-a
declares an indexed array,
.B \-A
an associative array,
.B \-i
an integer, whose assignments are evaluated as arithmetic,
.B \-l
and
.B \-u
lowercase and uppercase conversion,
.B \-r
a read-only variable, and
.B \-x
an exported one. A plus removes an attribute, except that a read-only
attribute stays.
.B \-t
is accepted without effect.
.B \-f
and
.B \-F
print functions with or without their definitions.
.B \-p
prints declarations, and without operands
.B \-p
or an attribute option lists every variable with those attributes. Without
options or operands, declare prints nothing. \-a with \-A returns 2, and a
missing variable or function, or an invalid identifier, returns 1.
.IP
.B \-n
makes a reference to the variable in its value, which may carry a subscript.
Expansion, assignment, append, export, and unset act on the target, following
chains, with the target's read-only, restricted, integer, and case rules.
${!name} gives the target,
.B unset \-n
and
.B +n
remove the reference, and [[ \-R name ]] is true for a reference with a value.
A reference without a value binds at its first assignment. A self-reference is
an error at top level. A circular chain expands as unset without a message, and
an assignment through it is an error. A
read-only variable, PATH, IFS, and a dynamic variable other than RANDOM and
SECONDS cannot become a reference.
.TP
\fBdirs\fR [\fB\-clpv\fR] [\fB+\fIN\fR|\fB\-\fIN\fR]
Print the current directory and the saved directory stack on one line, with
HOME shown as ~.
.B \-c
clears the stack,
.B \-l
prints full paths,
.B \-p
prints one entry per line, and
.B \-v
numbers them from 0.
.BI + N
prints the Nth entry from the top and
.BI \- N
the Nth from the bottom. An entry past the end returns 1.
.TP
\fBdisown\fR [\fB\-ahr\fR] [\fIjob\fR ...]
Remove each job, by default the current job, from the job table.
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
removes every job and
.BR \-r ", " \-\-running
every running job.
.BR \-h ", " \-\-nohup
keeps the job in the table. A missing job returns 1.
.TP
\fBecho\fR [\fB\-neE\fR] [\fIargument\fR ...]
Print the arguments separated by spaces and followed by a newline.
.B \-n
omits the newline,
.B \-e
interprets backslash escapes, and
.B \-E
does not. The
.B kosh
mood interprets escapes by default. The escapes are those of $'...', except
that an octal escape is \e0\fInnn\fR in the Bash moods, and \ec ends the
output. In the
.B kosh
and
.B bash
moods the options may be combined, as in \-ne, and any other argument that
begins with a dash is printed. In the
.B sh
mood, as in dash, only \-n is an option, escapes are interpreted by default,
\ee and \ex are not escapes, and \eu is.
.B xpg_echo
makes the Bash moods interpret escapes by default. On Windows, echo in the
.B kosh
mood leaves backslashes literal unless
.B \-e
is given. A write error returns 1, or 2 in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
\fBenable\fR [\fB\-adnps\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
Enable each builtin or bundled utility, or disable it with
.BR \-n .
A utility alias such as
.B egrep
is rejected and follows its utility. Each builtin word is separate, so
disabling
.B test
leaves
.B [
enabled. Lookup skips a disabled builtin and searches PATH and the bundled
utilities, and a disabled bundled utility never resolves as a command, though
the
.B koshkit
builtin still runs it. A disabled special builtin loses its special
properties. A subshell inherits the state, and its changes do not reach the
parent.
.IP
Without an operand, and with
.BR \-p ,
enable lists the enabled builtins and reachable utilities as
.B enable
commands,
.B \-n
the disabled ones, and
.B \-a
both.
.B \-s
limits a listing to special builtins. A builtin that the mood hides, such as
.B let
in the
.B sh
mood, is neither listed nor accepted. Koshka has no loadable builtins, so
.B \-f
with an operand and
.B \-d
return 1, and so does a name that is not a builtin or utility.
.TP
\fBeval\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Join the arguments with spaces and run the result as shell input in the
current shell. The status is that of the last command, or 0 for empty input. A
syntax error in the input is reported with an eval: location and returns 2,
and in the POSIX moods it ends a non-interactive shell with status 2.
.TP
\fBexec\fR [\fB\-cl\fR] [\fB\-a\fR \fIname\fR] [\fIcommand\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]]
Replace the shell with command. Without a command, apply the redirections to
the shell itself.
.B \-a
passes name as argument zero,
.B \-l
puts a dash before argument zero, and
.B \-c
runs the command with an empty environment. A command that is not found or
cannot be run ends a non-interactive shell with status 127 or 126, in every
mood, and returns that status in an interactive shell. A restricted shell
refuses exec.
.TP
\fBexit\fR [\fIn\fR]
Run the EXIT trap and exit with status n modulo 256, or with the status of the
last command. A non-numeric operand prints an error and returns 2 without
exiting, except in the
.B sh
mood, where the shell exits with status 2. More than one operand exits with
status 1.
.TP
\fBexport\fR [\fB\-fnp\fR] [\fIname\fR[\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR] ...]
Mark each variable for the environment of later commands, after assigning the
value when one is given.
.B \-n
removes the mark,
.B \-f
marks functions, and
.B \-p
or no operand prints the exported variables. The
.B kosh
and
.B sh
moods print them as export commands and the Bash moods as declare \-x
commands. An invalid identifier and a read-only variable return 1.
.TP
\fBfalse\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Return 1.
.TP
\fBfc\fR [\fB\-e\fR \fIeditor\fR] [\fB\-lnr\fR] [\fIfirst\fR [\fIlast\fR]]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBfc \-s\fR [\fIold\fB=\fInew\fR ...] [\fIcommand\fR]
.PD
Edit and run history events. A number selects an event, a negative number is
relative to the current position, and text selects the newest event with that
prefix. The default is the previous event.
.B \-l
lists events, the previous sixteen by default,
.B \-n
omits numbers, and
.B \-r
reverses the order. The editor comes from
.BR \-e ,
FCEDIT, or EDITOR, and defaults to vi, or ed in the POSIX moods. An editor of
.B \-
runs the event unedited.
.B fc \-s
applies each literal replacement to one event, prints it to standard error,
runs it, and returns its status. A text that matches no event returns 1.
.TP
\fBfg\fR [\fIjob\fR]
Resume a job in the foreground, by default the current job, and return its
status. A missing job returns 1, or 2 in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
\fBgetopts\fR \fIoptstring\fR \fIname\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Parse the options in the arguments, or in the positional parameters. Each call
stores the next option letter in name and its argument in OPTARG, and OPTIND
holds the index of the next argument. A letter followed by a colon in
optstring takes an argument. An unknown option sets name to ? and prints a
diagnostic. OPTERR=0 silences the diagnostics. A leading colon in optstring
also silences them, and then an unknown option stores the letter in OPTARG and
a missing argument sets name to :. The status is 0 while options remain and
1 at their end.
.TP
\fBhash\fR [\fB\-rRt\fR] [\fB\-p\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
Find each name in PATH and remember its location.
.B \-r
forgets every location, and
.B \-R
also searches PATH again.
.BI "\-p " path
records path for each name.
.B \-t
prints the remembered location of each name. A name that cannot be found
returns 1.
.TP
\fBhelp\fR [\fB\-dms\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
Print the help of each builtin, as its
.B \-\-help
flag does.
.B \-d
prints the short description,
.B \-s
the synopsis, and
.B \-m
a manual page. Without a name, list the builtins. A name that is not a builtin
returns 1.
.TP
\fBhistory\fR [\fB\-cnrwaps\fR] [\fB\-d\fR \fIselector\fR] [\fIoperand\fR]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBhistory\fR \fB\-S\fR|\fB\-\-sync\fR
.PD
Without a maintenance option, list the events, limited to the newest N with a
numeric operand.
.B \-r
loads the history file, or the file operand, into the list, and
.B \-n
loads only events not yet loaded.
.B \-w
writes the list to the history file or replaces the file operand.
.B \-a
appends the events of the private list that the file lacks.
.B \-S
replaces the private list with a snapshot of the shared file.
.B \-c
clears the private list and leaves the file intact.
.B \-p
prints its operands without storing them, and
.B \-s
stores them as one event.
.B \-d
deletes an event number, a negative offset from the newest event, or an
inclusive range, and an interactive shell also removes it from the file. An
event out of range returns 1. A restricted shell refuses file operands.
.TP
\fBjobs\fR [\fB\-lnprs\fR] [\fIjob\fR ...]
List the jobs, as described under
.BR "JOB CONTROL" .
.B \-l
adds the process id,
.B \-p
prints only process ids,
.B \-r
and
.B \-s
limit the list to running or stopped jobs, and
.B \-n
to jobs that changed since the last report. A missing job returns 1, or 2 in
the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
\fBkill\fR [\fB\-s\fR \fIsignal\fR | \fB\-n\fR \fInum\fR | \fB\-\fIsignal\fR] \fIpid\fR|\fIjob\fR ...
.PD 0
.TP
\fBkill \-l\fR [\fIsignal\fR|\fIstatus\fR ...]
.PD
Send a signal, by default SIGTERM, to each process or job. A signal is a
number or a name with or without SIG.
.B \-l
lists the signals, or translates each number, status above 128, or name. No
operand returns 2, and an invalid signal or a process that cannot be signaled
returns 1. In the
.B sh
mood an invalid signal returns 2.
.TP
\fBkoshconf create\fR \fIPRESET\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBkoshconf set\fR \fIOPTION VALUE\fR [\fB\-\-persist\fR]
.TP
\fBkoshconf get\fR \fIOPTION\fR
.TP
\fBkoshconf list\fR [\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR]
.TP
\fBkoshconf load\fR \fIENCODED\fR
.TP
\fBkoshconf save\fR [\fB\-\-persist\fR]
.PD
Read and change the Koshka settings.
.B create
writes the default file of the
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash ,
or
.B sh
preset to the user settings file, with every setting except the mood
commented out, and refuses to replace an existing file without
.BR \-\-force .
It also writes the default user rc script when that is missing.
.B set
changes the option for the session, and with
.B \-\-persist
also rewrites its line in the user settings file, saving the previous file as
.IR kosh.conf.bak .
.B get
prints the value.
.B list
prints every option as
.IR name = value
in alphabetical order, and
.B \-a
adds a line for each alias and for the
.B set
options hashall and nolog, which only
.B set
accepts.
.B load
applies a
.B KOSHCONF
value to the session.
.B save
prints the current mood and interactive options as a
.B KOSHCONF
value, so a kosh started with
.B KOSHCONF=$(koshconf save)
adopts them. With
.BR \-\-persist ,
it writes the user settings file instead, holding each setting that differs
from the system file or, where the system file does not set it, from the
default of the mood.
.IP
A boolean is on or off, also accepted as true, false, 1, or 0. An unknown
option or value, or a file that cannot be read or written, returns 1.
Invalid command syntax returns 2. An interactive shell reports the file it
wrote on
standard error. A restricted shell refuses create, set, load, and save
\-\-persist.
.TP
\fBkoshkit\fR [\fB\-\-color\fR \fIwhen\fR] [\fIutility\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]]
Run a bundled utility, as described under
.BR "KOSHKIT UTILITIES" .
.TP
\fBlet\fR \fIexpression\fR ...
Evaluate each expression, as described under
.BR "ARITHMETIC EVALUATION" .
The status is 0 when the last value is nonzero and 1 when it is zero. No
expression returns 1. The
.B sh
mood has no let.
.TP
\fBlocal\fR [\fB\-aAilnprux\fR] [\fIname\fR[\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR] ...]
Make variables local to the running function, with the attributes of the same
options of
.BR declare .
.B local \-n
passes a variable of the caller by reference, and a
.B \-
operand saves the
.B set
options until the function returns.
.B \-p
lists the local variables. Outside a function local returns 1, and in the
.B sh
mood it ends a non-interactive shell with status 2.
.TP
\fBlogout\fR [\fIn\fR]
In a login shell, run the EXIT trap and exit with the optional status. In
another shell, report an error and return 1.
.TP
\fBmapfile\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIarray\fR]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBreadarray\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIarray\fR]
.PD
Store the lines of standard input in an indexed array, MAPFILE by default.
.B \-t
strips delimiters,
.B \-n
limits the count, with 0 for every line,
.B \-s
skips leading lines,
.B \-O
sets the first index,
.B \-d
uses the first byte of its value as delimiter, NUL when empty,
.B \-u
reads a descriptor, and
.B \-C
runs a callback with the index and the line every
.B \-c
lines, 5000 by default.
.TP
\fBnewgrp\fR [\fIgroup\fR]
Replace the shell with the newgrp program found in PATH, which starts a new
shell with the group. A missing program returns 127, and one that cannot run
returns 126. A restricted shell refuses newgrp.
.TP
\fBpopd\fR [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB+\fIN\fR|\fB\-\fIN\fR]
Remove the top entry, or entry N, from the directory stack and change to the
new top.
.B \-n
removes the entry below the current directory without changing directory. An
empty stack returns 1.
.TP
\fBprintf\fR [\fB\-v\fR \fIvariable\fR] \fIformat\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Print the arguments under the control of format, which is reused while
arguments remain.
.B \-v
assigns the result to a variable or array element. The format accepts the C
conversions d, i, o, u, x, X, e, E, f, F, g, G, c, and s with flags, width, and
precision, * for a width or precision from an argument, %b for an argument with
backslash escapes, %q for an argument quoted for reuse, and %(\fIformat\fR)T for
a time in seconds, formatted by strftime, with \-1 for the current time. An
argument that is not a valid number prints 0 and makes the status 1. A
missing format returns 2. A field width or precision over 16777216 is an error,
and that conversion prints nothing.
.IP
The numeric locale is the first nonempty value of LC_ALL, LC_NUMERIC, and LANG
at each call. Floating operands use its decimal point, and the
.B \(aq
flag groups digits with its thousands separator. The C and POSIX locales and
every Windows locale use a dot and no grouping. The
.B sh
mood rejects \-v and prints %(\fIformat\fR)T as written.
.TP
\fBpushd\fR [\fB\-n\fR] [\fIdir\fR|\fB+\fIN\fR|\fB\-\fIN\fR]
Push dir onto the directory stack and change to it. Without an operand, swap
the two top entries, and with
.BI + N
or
.BI \- N
rotate the stack so that entry N is on top.
.B \-n
adds dir below the current directory without changing directory. A missing
directory or an empty stack returns 1.
.TP
\fBpwd\fR [\fB\-LPW\fR]
Print the working directory.
.B \-L
prints the logical path, the default unless the
.B physical
option is on, and
.B \-P
the path with symbolic links resolved.
.B \-W
prints the physical path in Windows form with forward slashes, as MSYS does,
and acts as
.B \-P
elsewhere.
.TP
\fBread\fR [\fB\-ers\fR] [\fB\-a\fR \fIarray\fR] [\fB\-d\fR \fIdelim\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-N\fR \fIcount\fR] [\fB\-p\fR \fIprompt\fR] [\fB\-t\fR \fItimeout\fR] [\fB\-u\fR \fIfd\fR] [\fB\-q\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
Read a line from standard input and split it into fields at IFS. Each name
receives one field and the last name receives the rest of the line. Without a
name the line goes to REPLY.
.B \-r
keeps backslashes, and otherwise a trailing backslash continues the line.
.B \-a
splits into an indexed array,
.B \-d
sets the delimiter (empty for NUL),
.B \-n
sets the maximum byte count,
.B \-N
reads exactly that many bytes unsplit, ignoring the delimiter,
.B \-t
sets a timeout in seconds, which may be fractional,
.B \-p
prints a prompt at a terminal,
.B \-s
disables echo,
.B \-u
reads a descriptor, and
.B \-q
reads one byte and returns 0 for y or Y.
.B \-e
is accepted without effect. The status is 1 at the end of input, even when a
partial line was stored, and 142 after a timeout. The
.B sh
mood requires a name and rejects every option except \-r and \-p.
.TP
\fBreadonly\fR [\fB\-aAfp\fR] [\fIname\fR[\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR] ...]
Mark variables read-only, after assigning the value when one is given.
.B \-p
or no operand lists them reusably,
.B \-a
and
.B \-A
treat operands as arrays, and
.B \-f
marks functions read-only, or lists them with their bodies when given alone.
Assigning a read-only variable is an error, which ends a non-interactive shell
in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
\fBreturn\fR [\fIn\fR]
Leave the running function or sourced file with status n modulo 256, or with
the status of the last command. Outside a function or sourced file return
reports an error and returns 2. A non-numeric operand returns 2.
.TP
\fBset\fR [\fB\-+abefhkmnprtuvxBCEHPTIARNSGW\fR] [\fB\-+o\fR \fIoption\fR] [\fB\-M\fR \fImood\fR] [\fB\-L\fR \fImoods\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] [\fIargument\fR ...]
Change options and positional parameters. A minus enables an option and a plus
disables it. Arguments after the options, or after \-\-, replace the
positional parameters, and \- also ends the options. Without arguments, set
prints the shell variables as sorted assignments.
.B set \-o
.I option
accepts only Bash spellings. Without an option,
.B set \-o
prints the states and
.B set +o
prints reusable commands. A long option such as
.B \-\-mood
is rejected with a pointer to
.BR koshconf .
.IP
The letters are \-a (allexport), \-b (notify, which reports a finished job at
once while the editor waits), \-e (errexit), \-f (noglob), \-h (hashall,
accepted without effect), \-k (keyword, which places assignment arguments in
the command environment), \-m (monitor, job control), \-n (noexec), \-p
(privileged), \-t (onecmd, which stops after one top-level command), \-u
(nounset), \-v (verbose), \-x (xtrace), \-B (braceexpand), \-C (noclobber), \-E
(errtrace), \-H (histexpand), \-P (physical), and \-T (functrace). Options
without a letter are emacs, vi, history, ignoreeof, interactive\-comments,
nolog (accepted without effect), pipefail, and posix, which selects the
.B bash-posix
mood, while
.B set +o posix
selects
.B bash
from the
.B bash-posix
or
.B sh
mood. The options are described under
.BR "OPTION REGISTRY" .
.IP
The Koshka forms
.BI "set \-M " MOOD
and
.BI "set \-L " MOOD,...
select the mood and source the startup files of other moods as the
command-line options do. Without a value they print the mood or the
initialized moods.
.BR \-I ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-N ,
.BR \-S ,
and
.B \-G
set interpreter.mimic_shebang, debug.print_syntax_tree,
debug.print_lexed_word_escapes, debug.report_every_exit_code,
debug.print_evaluation_statistics, and debug.print_memory_report_at_exit, and
.BR \-W ,
.BR \-WW ,
and
.B \-WWW
set optimizer.warning_level. Every other Koshka setting belongs to
.BR koshconf .
.B \-r
turns restricted mode on, and
.B +r
fails with status 1. An unknown option returns 1 in the Bash moods, ends a
non-interactive shell with status 1 in the
.B kosh
mood, and with status 2 in the
.B sh
mood. In the
.B kosh
mood, a value other than the one held for nounset, pipefail, failglob, or
nullglob is an error.
.TP
\fBshift\fR [\fIn\fR]
Discard the first n positional parameters, by default 1. A count above $# or
below 0 returns 1, and in the
.B sh
mood it ends a non-interactive shell with status 2.
.TP
\fBshopt\fR [\fB\-pqsuo\fR] [\fIoption\fR ...]
Show or change the
.B shopt
options, which are described under
.BR "OPTION REGISTRY" .
.B \-s
enables and
.B \-u
disables each option, or without one lists the options that are on or off.
Without \-s or \-u, shopt prints the state of each option, or of all of them,
and returns 1 when one is off.
.B \-p
prints reusable shopt commands,
.B \-o
selects the
.B set \-o
options, and
.B \-q
prints nothing. An unknown option returns 1 after the valid ones are applied.
.TP
\fBslurp\fR [\fB\-e\fR|\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-V\fR] [\fB+\fILINE\fR] \fIFILE\fR ...
Edit each FILE in the line editor, one after another. A missing FILE starts
empty, and Ctrl\-C ends the whole run.
.BI + LINE
opens the next FILE at that line.
.BR \-e ", " \-\-emacs
selects the emacs key set,
.BR \-v ", " \-\-vi
the vi key set in insert mode, and
.BR \-V ", " \-\-vi\-normal
the vi key set in normal mode on the first line. The bundled utilities vi and
emacs run slurp \-V and slurp \-e. The session runs on the alternate screen. A
shell script, recognized by its shebang or extension, keeps syntax
highlighting, and the format commands change only such a file.
.IP
The top row repeats the prompt and the command, the file fills the rows below,
and the last two rows show the file kind, line endings, size, and caret
position, then the key set and the last save result or the keys of the key
menu. A hint, a message, or a command line that needs more rows takes up to
eight while it shows. Enter starts a new line, and Up and Down do not recall
history. Tab inserts a tab at the start of a line or in a file that is not a
shell script, and completes otherwise. Tabs and trailing blanks are drawn
shaded. The mouse moves the caret, selects by dragging, and scrolls with the
wheel. With the emacs key set, Shift with a movement key also selects, as
described under
.BR "Emacs mode keys" .
.IP
Ctrl\-X s, Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-S,
.BR :w ,
and
.B :write
save and keep the session open.
.BR :wq ,
.BR :wq! ,
.BR :wqa ,
.BR :wqa! ,
and
.B :x
save and leave when the save succeeds.
.BR :q ,
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-C, and Ctrl\-D on an empty buffer leave without saving, and
Ctrl\-C leaves with status 130. While the buffer has unsaved changes,
.B :q
refuses, the other keys and M\-x kill\-kosh must be pressed twice, and
.B :q!
leaves at once.
.BR : N
and
.B :$
move to line N and the last line. Ctrl\-S and Ctrl\-R, or
.B /
and
.BR ? ,
find text as it is typed.
.B help
in M\-x and
.B :help
list the commands.
.IP
A save writes a complete copy beside the file and renames it over the file,
keeping its mode and following a symbolic link. A file that another user owns,
a file with several links, or a file in a directory that neither the user nor
root owns, or that others can write without the sticky bit, is rewritten in
place, keeping its owner, group, and links, after a private backup copy is
made in the home or temporary directory. The copy is removed after a
successful write and reported after a failed one, and without a copy nothing
is written. A save is refused when another file has replaced the loaded one.
A failed save is reported and the edits stay open. CRLF line endings and the
final newline are kept.
.IP
An empty operand, a directory, a special or unreadable file, a file with a NUL
byte or invalid UTF-8, and a file over 16 MiB are refused with status 1, and
the next FILE still opens. A restricted shell, or standard input or output that
is not a terminal, is refused with status 1. A missing FILE, a
.BI + LINE
without a FILE, or more than one mode option returns 2.
.TP
\fBsuspend\fR [\fB\-f\fR]
Stop the shell until it receives SIGCONT. A login shell refuses without
.BR \-f .
.TP
\fBtime\fR [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-posix\fR] [\fB\-R\fR] \fIpipeline\fR
A reserved word, described under
.BR Pipelines .
.TP
\fBtimes\fR
Print the user and system times of the shell, then of its children.
.TP
\fBtrap\fR [\fB\-lp\fR] [[\fIaction\fR] \fIcondition\fR ...]
Set action to run when the shell receives each signal or meets each condition.
The conditions are the signals by number or by name with or without SIG, EXIT
or 0 at exit, ERR after a failing command where errexit would act, DEBUG
before each simple command, and RETURN at the end of a function or sourced
file. An empty action ignores the signal, and an action of \- resets the
default. Without arguments, and with
.BR \-p ,
trap prints the actions as reusable trap commands, and
.B \-l
lists the signals. An invalid signal returns 1. A trap action runs with the
status of the command before it, and it does not change that status. The
.B sh
mood rejects ERR, RETURN, \-l, and \-p.
.TP
\fBtype\fR [\fB\-aPptV\fR] \fIname\fR ...
Describe how each name is interpreted as a command.
.B \-t
prints one of alias, keyword, function, builtin, or file,
.B \-p
and
.B \-P
print the path of a program,
.B \-a
prints every interpretation, and
.B \-V
prints everything the shell holds about each word, such as a function's
definition site and body or the description of a builtin or utility. The Bash
moods report a function as "is a function" followed by its body, and the other
moods as "is a shell function". A name that is not found returns 1, and 127 in
the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
\fBulimit\fR [\fB\-HSa\fR] [\fB\-cdflmnpstuvw\fR] [\fIlimit\fR]
Show or set a resource limit, by default the file size limit \-f.
.B \-H
and
.B \-S
select the hard or the soft limit, and
.B \-a
prints every limit. The resources are \-c (core size), \-d (data size), \-f
(file size), \-l (locked memory), \-m (resident set), \-n (open files), \-p
(pipe size), \-s (stack size), \-t (CPU seconds), \-u (processes), \-v (virtual
memory), and \-w (file locks).
.B \-a
omits the pipe size and adds the real-time priority. A limit is a number or
unlimited, and operands after the limit are ignored. An invalid limit, or one
above the hard limit, returns 1.
.TP
\fBumask\fR [\fB\-pS\fR] [\fImask\fR]
Show or set the file creation mask. A mask is octal or symbolic, as in
u=rwx,g=rx,o=.
.B \-S
prints the mask symbolically, and
.B \-p
as a reusable command. An invalid mask returns 1.
.TP
\fBunalias\fR [\fB\-a\fR] \fIname\fR ...
Remove each alias, or every alias with
.BR \-a .
An unknown alias returns 1.
.TP
\fBunset\fR [\fB\-fnv\fR] \fIname\fR ...
Remove each variable or array element, or each function with
.BR \-f .
Without an option a variable is removed, or a function when no such variable
exists.
.B \-n
removes a name reference itself. A read-only variable returns 1, and an unset
name succeeds.
.TP
\fBwait\fR [\fB\-fn\fR] [\fB\-p\fR \fIvariable\fR] [\fIpid\fR|\fIjob\fR ...]
Wait for each process or job and return the status of the last one, or wait
for every child and return 0.
.B \-n
waits for the next job to finish among the operands, or among all jobs, and
returns 127 when none is left.
.BI \-p " variable"
stores the process whose status is returned, or unsets the variable.
.B \-f
waits until each job terminates. A stopped job returns 128 plus the stop
signal, and
.B wait \-n
skips it. A process that is not a child of the shell, or a job that does not
exist, returns 127 with a diagnostic, or without one in the
.B sh
mood, and a missing job number returns 2 in the
.B sh
mood.
.TP
\fBz\fR [\fIquery\fR ...]
Change to a directory that interactive shells have visited. A single operand
that is an existing directory is used as it is. Otherwise the operands form one
case-insensitive substring query, ranked by visit count and recency. With no
operand, z changes to the home directory. z prints the directory it changes to
on standard output. No matching directory returns 1.
.SH KOSHKIT UTILITIES
The
.B koshkit
builtin runs a bundled utility, and an unknown utility is an error. With no
utility it groups the catalog under POSIX and Koshka headings.
.B koshkit \-\-list
prints the utilities one per line, and
.B koshkit \-\-list\-flags
prints each utility and alias with its short and long flags.
.PP
The bundled set is basename, bc, cal, calc, cat, chgrp, chmod, chown, cksum,
cmp, comm, cp, csplit, cut, date, df, diff, dirname, du, env, evil, evildisk,
evilfiles, evilfs, evilio, eviliso, evillogs, evilnet, evilps, evilss, expand,
expr, file, find, flock, fold, fuser, getconf, goodcore, goodfsw, goodnode,
goodstat, grep, head, id, killall, less, link, ln, locale, logger, logname, ls,
make,
man, meow, mkdir, mkfifo, mknod, more, mv, nice, nl, nohup, nproc, od, paste,
pathchk, pkill, pr, printenv, ps, readlink, realpath, renice, retry, rm, rmdir,
sed, seq, sleep, slurp, sort, split, stat, strings, stty, sync, tabs, tail, tee,
timeout, touch, tput, tr, tsort, tty, uname, unexpand, uniq, unlink, watch, wc,
which, who, whoami, xargs, and yes, with the aliases egrep and fgrep for grep
and vi and emacs for slurp.
.PP
Every utility supports
.BR \-\-help ,
and
.B \-\-version
when invoked under its own name. Options may come before and after operands,
and
.B \-\-
ends them. calc, env, expr, flock, nice, nohup, retry, timeout, watch, and
xargs end their options at the first operand, so the command after it keeps its
own options.
.PP
A utility with a styled report, such as cal, cat, du, ls, watch, meow, and the
evil and good utilities, uses color only when its output is a terminal and
NO_COLOR is empty or unset.
.B koshkit \-\-color
.I when
selects always, never, or auto for the utility that follows, and diff and grep
also take
.BR \-\-color .
tabs and tput write escape sequences wherever their output goes.
.PP
A utility returns 0 on success, 1 when an operand fails, and 2 for a usage
error, such as an unknown flag or a missing operand. A failure on one file is
reported and the utility goes on with the next. In the
.B kosh
mood a usage error ends the script, as described under
.BR "SHELL ERRORS" .
An unknown utility returns 1. A diagnostic names the utility, as in Koshkit
`seq`: for the builtin and seq: for a utility started under its own name.
.PP
A confirmation question, such as one from
.BR "rm \-i" ,
.BR "cp \-i" ,
.BR "mv \-i" ,
or
.BR assimilate ,
is written to standard error after the shell prefix and followed by [y/N] or
[Y/n], the default in capitals. The answer is read from standard input up to a
newline. An empty line takes the default, an answer beginning with y or Y
confirms, and any other answer or end of input declines.
.PP
When direct koshkit lookup is enabled, a PATH program takes precedence over a
bundled utility. Started under a utility name, the executable runs that
utility. The whole name must match with its case kept, and on Windows the
extension is removed first. Started as
.BR koshkit ,
the link that
.B make install
places beside
.BR kosh ,
it runs the utility in its first operand, as in
.BR "koshkit ls \-l" ,
and otherwise acts as the koshkit builtin. Either form skips shell startup,
settings, and KOSH_FLAGS.
.TP
.B basename
Print the final path component, minus an optional suffix.
.BR \-a ", " \-\-multiple
treats every operand as a path,
.BR \-s ", " \-\-suffix
removes a suffix from each and implies
.BR \-a ,
and
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
ends entries with NUL.
.TP
.B bc
Evaluate expressions, assignments, functions, arrays, control flow, input and
output bases, and scale, from the file operands, or from standard input when
there are none.
.B \-l
adds the functions s (sine), c (cosine), a (arctangent), l (natural
logarithm), and e (exponential) and sets scale to 20, and
.B \-q
suppresses the banner. Division by zero is reported, evaluation continues, and
the status is 1.
.TP
.B cal
Print a Gregorian month or year with weeks starting on Sunday.
.BR \-a ", " \-\-today
starts weeks on Monday and prints the date in words below.
.TP
.B calc
Evaluate arithmetic operands with arbitrary-precision integers and finite
decimals.
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
and
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pipe
read expressions interactively or from a pipe. The functions are abs, atan,
ceil, cmp, cos, exp, fact, fib, floor, frac, gcd, int, iseven, isint, isodd,
length, lcm, ln, max, min, scale, sgn, sin, and sqrt. Ctrl\-R searches the calc
history.
.TP
.B cat
Concatenate files or standard input.
.B \-n
numbers lines across files,
.B \-b
numbers nonempty lines,
.B \-s
squeezes empty runs,
.B \-v
shows control bytes in ^ notation and high bytes with M\-,
.B \-E
marks line ends with $ and a CR before a newline as ^M,
.B \-T
shows tabs as ^I, and
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-t ,
and
.B \-A
act as
.BR \-vE ,
.BR \-vT ,
and
.BR \-vET .
.B \-u
is accepted.
.B \-\-syntax\-highlighting
colors shell source on a terminal.
.TP
.BR chgrp ", " chown
Change the group, or the owner and optional group, of each file.
.B \-R
recurses,
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-L ,
and
.B \-P
select which links are followed,
.B \-h
changes a link itself, and
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
keeps recursion on one file system.
.TP
.B chmod
Apply symbolic or octal modes.
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recursive
recurses without following links, and
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
skips other file systems.
.TP
.B cksum
Print the POSIX CRC checksum and byte count of each file or standard input,
followed by the file.
.TP
.B cmp
Compare two files byte by byte. A difference prints the first differing byte
and line and returns 1, equal files return 0, and an unreadable file returns 2.
.BR \-l ", " \-\-verbose
lists every differing byte with both values in octal, and
.BR \-s ", " \-\-silent
prints nothing. When one file is a prefix of the other, cmp reports the end of
the shorter file on standard error and returns 1. A file of \- is standard
input.
.TP
.B comm
Merge two sorted files into columns of lines only in the first, only in the
second, and in both;
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-2 ,
and
.B \-3
suppress them. A line out of order after an unpaired line is reported once per
file with status 1;
.B \-\-check\-order
stops at the first line out of order, and
.B \-\-nocheck\-order
skips the check, the later one winning.
.B \-\-output\-delimiter
sets the column separator (empty for NUL),
.B \-\-total
appends column counts, and
.B \-z
uses NUL line ends.
.TP
.B cp
Copy files and trees with
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.BR \-v .
A recursive copy keeps links as links;
.B \-L
follows every link,
.B \-H
only links on the command line, and
.B \-P
none. A link leading back into a directory being copied is reported as cyclic
and skipped with status 1.
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
copies a directory on another file system as an empty directory.
.TP
.B csplit
Split a file into pieces at each pattern, and print the byte count of each
piece. A pattern is a line number, a /\fIregex\fR/ that starts a piece, or a
%\fIregex\fR% that skips the lines before the match, and a regex may take a
signed line offset. A following {\fIn\fR} repeats the pattern n times and {*}
repeats it until the input ends. The pieces are xx00, xx01, and so on.
.BR \-f ", " \-\-prefix
replaces xx,
.BR \-n ", " \-\-digits
sets the number of digits,
.BR \-s ", " \-\-silent
suppresses the counts, and
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep\-files
keeps the pieces after an error. A line number past the input or a regex that
does not match returns 1.
.TP
.B cut
Select bytes, characters, or fields with
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
or
.BR \-f .
.B \-d
sets the delimiter,
.B \-s
skips lines without one,
.B \-\-complement
inverts the selection,
.BI \-\-output\-delimiter= str
joins selections with str, and
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero\-terminated
uses NUL line ends. With
.BR \-b ,
.B \-n
leaves out a multibyte character that the byte range splits. An invalid list
or a delimiter longer than one byte returns 1.
.TP
.B date
Print the date and time, or format them with a +\fIformat\fR operand of
strftime conversions.
.BR \-u ", " \-\-utc
uses UTC. An operand that would set the clock is refused.
.TP
.B df
Report mounted file systems or those of the operands in a right-aligned table.
.B \-k
uses 1024-byte blocks,
.B \-P
the POSIX header,
.BR \-h ", " \-\-human\-readable
powers of 1024, and
.BR \-H ", " \-\-si
powers of 1000. Unreadable file systems are reported before the table.
.TP
.B diff
Compare two files line by line.
.B \-u
selects unified output,
.B \-w
ignores whitespace,
.B \-a
forces text, and up to two
.B \-L
options set the unified labels.
.BR \-\-color [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
takes auto (the default), always, or never. The status is 0 for equal files, 1
for different files, and 2 for an error.
.TP
.B dirname
Print the directory part of each path.
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
ends entries with NUL.
.TP
.B du
Print every file and directory below each path, or the current directory, in
descending size order without following links.
.B \-s
prints totals only,
.B \-h
human-readable sizes,
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
skips other file systems,
.B \-\-tree
prints a tree, and
.BR \-T ", " \-\-top\-largest
a tree of the largest entries and their ancestors. Permission errors are
warnings, other read failures are errors, and either makes the status 1.
.TP
.B env
Run a command with temporary assignments, or print the environment.
.B \-i
starts empty, and each
.B \-u
removes a variable. The command is found through its PATH, or /usr/bin:/bin
without one.
.TP
.BR expand ", " unexpand
Convert tabs to spaces, or blanks to tabs.
.B \-t
takes ascending tab stops separated by commas or blanks, or one interval; a
last value /N adds a stop at each multiple of N, and +N one every N columns,
after the last explicit stop. The default is every eight columns.
.B "unexpand \-a"
also converts blanks after the first nonblank, and
.B \-t
implies it.
.TP
.BR evil " and the evil utilities"
.B evil
prints system identity, uptime, processor, memory, swap, file system, process,
and network information;
.B \-a
adds details,
.B \-s
selects identity fields,
.B \-u
lists local users.
.B evildisk
prints capacity, file system identity, and disk error and retry totals, and
with
.B \-a
labels, UUIDs, error counters, and SMART data where available.
.B evilfiles
lists open files, filtered by
.B \-p
(process),
.B \-u
(owner),
.B \-c
(command prefix),
.B \-i
(sockets), and
.B \-t
(terse pids), with
.BR \-w ", " \-\-wide
for unshortened columns.
.B evilfs
prints mounted file systems.
.B evillogs
prints core dump settings, dump reports, and log directories, or only one with
.B \-\-cores
or
.BR \-\-logs .
.B eviliso
prints namespaces
.RB ( \-n ),
cgroups
.RB ( \-c ),
login sessions
.RB ( \-s ),
remote sockets
.RB ( \-r ),
container runtime data
.RB ( \-k ),
.BR \-\-container ,
and
.B \-\-kubernetes
sections, all of them without a selector, with
.B \-a
for detailed rows. The Kubernetes section reads KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST, the
service-account namespace file, and kubepods cgroups.
.B evilss
prints TCP, UDP, and Unix sockets.
.BR \-l " (listening), " \-a " (all), " \-t " (TCP), " \-u " (UDP), " \-x
(Unix),
.BR \-4 ", and " \-6
select sockets,
.B \-p
adds the owning process,
.B \-n
keeps addresses numeric, and
.B \-H
omits the header.
.B evilps
and
.B evildisk
take optional process ids and files, and
.B evilfiles
optional paths, to limit their reports.
.IP
.B evilio
prints memory, swap, and cumulative disk counters;
.B \-a
adds processor, paging, scheduler, pressure, disk activity, and swap rates
from a one-second sample,
.BR \-h ", " \-\-human\-readable
(the default) uses binary units, and
.BR \-\-ps ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.BI \- NUMBER
print per-process counters for every process, a limited table, one process, or
NUMBER rows.
.B evilnet
prints IPv4 and IPv6 addresses;
.B \-a
adds traffic, errors, drops, and TCP counters,
.B \-f
TCP failure and loss counters only, and
.BR \-t ", " \-\-traffic
the traffic table only.
.B evilps
prints processes as a tree;
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-U ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-M ,
and
.BR \-w ", " \-\-wide
show pids, sort numerically, show every field, command lines, owners, CPU,
memory, and full-length lines, and
.BI \- NUMBER
limits the rows.
.IP
For evilio, evilnet, evilps, and evilss,
.BR \-l ", " \-\-live [= \fIN\fR ]
(only
.B \-\-live
for evilss) refreshes every N seconds, 0.5 by default, on the alternate screen
until q (status 0) or an interrupt (status 130); a tall frame scrolls with j,
k, the arrow and page keys, g, G, and the mouse wheel. In evilio, evilnet, and
evilps,
.BR \-\-cumulative [= \fIM\fR ]
(\fB\-C\fR in evilps) reports the change over M seconds, 1 by default, or a
rolling M-second window with
.BR \-\-live .
.B \-\-sort
orders rows by a metric or its shortest unambiguous prefix: pid, read, write,
read-ops, and write-ops for processes; also busy, read-latency,
write-latency, average-queue, queue, errors, and retries for disks; name,
bytes, packets, errors, or drops for traffic; and name, pid, cpu, or memory in
evilps, where s cycles the key and / searches in live view.
.TP
.B expr
Evaluate an expression whose operators and operands are separate arguments and
print the result. The operators are |, &, =, !=, <, <=, >, >=, +, \-, *, /, %,
and :, which matches a basic regular expression anchored at the start and
prints the first group or the match length. The status is 0 for a result that
is neither 0 nor empty and 1 otherwise. An invalid expression, integer
overflow, and division by zero are errors with status 1, and a missing
expression returns 2.
.TP
.B file
Classify file kinds and contents.
.BR \-h ", " \-\-no\-dereference
classifies a link and
.BR \-L ", " \-\-dereference
its target,
.BR \-i ", " \-\-regular\-only
skips content tests,
.BR \-m ", " \-\-magic\-file
adds a magic database, and
.BR \-M ", " \-\-magic\-only
uses only the given ones.
.BR \-d ", " \-\-default\-tests
selects the default tests. The kinds include text, data, directory, the
special files, ELF executable, common image formats, script text executable,
and symbolic link to a target. A file that cannot be opened returns 1.
.TP
.B find
Walk sorted trees with
.BR \-name ,
.BR \-iname ,
.BR \-type ,
.BR \-maxdepth ,
.BR \-mindepth ,
.BR \-print ,
and
.BR \-print0 .
.B \-xdev
and
.B \-mount
stay on one file system.
.B \-exec
.IR command " ... " ;
runs once per path with {} replaced, and
.B \-exec
.IR command " {} " +
runs on batches of paths.
.TP
.B flock
Run a command while holding a lock on a file or directory; the command inherits
the descriptor.
.BR \-n ", " \-\-nonblock
fails with status 1 instead of waiting, and
.B \-\-transaction\-held\-lock
keeps the lock after the launching process exits.
.TP
.B fold
Wrap the lines of each file or standard input at 80 columns, or at the width
of
.BR \-w ", " \-\-width .
A tab advances to the next multiple of eight.
.BR \-s ", " \-\-spaces
breaks after the last blank when one fits, and
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bytes
counts bytes.
.TP
.B fuser
Print the pids that use each file, with
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.BR \-u .
.TP
.B getconf
Print a system or string variable, or a path variable for a path; an
unsupported one prints undefined.
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
prints every variable, and
.BR \-v ", " \-\-specification
selects a POSIX environment.
.TP
.B goodcore
Capture a running process with
.BR \-p ,
through gcore or lldb, or package the core given as an operand.
.B \-b
gives the executable of the core, and without it goodcore infers the
executable from the output of file(1) and returns 1 when it cannot find it.
Capture fails when neither gcore nor lldb is available. The archive holds the core in dump/core, the executable and its libraries under
root/, and debug-gdb.sh and debug-lldb.sh, which open the core in gdb or lldb
with root/ as the system root and pass their arguments to the debugger. The
archive is goodcore\-\fIcore\fR.tar.zst, or .tar.gz when zstd is missing, or
the path of
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output ,
and
.B \-\-no\-compress
writes a plain tar.
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
prints only errors. The host tar and zstd create the archive, and gdb finds the
libraries.
.TP
.B goodfsw
Report path changes by scanning metadata, at most once per latency period. On
Linux a scan follows an inotify event and a quiet tree is rescanned every 30
periods; elsewhere, or when a watch fails, a scan runs every period. Each
record holds the scan time, a numeric event mask, and the path.
.BR \-h ", " \-\-human\-readable
uses timestamps and event labels, and
.BR \-m ", " \-\-machine\-readable
the numeric form;
.B \-\-timezone
and
.B \-\-precision
format human timestamps.
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-\-event\-flags ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.B \-e
select recursion, appended labels, one file system, one batch, the latency,
and an excluded substring.
.TP
.B goodnode
Print inode identity, allocation, file system capabilities, verification state,
and a CRC32C checksum for each path.
.B \-i
locates an inode beneath the
.B \-r
path, and
.B \-\-verify
runs the native file system verification where supported.
.TP
.B goodstat
Print file metadata as a labeled report.
.B \-L
follows links,
.B \-c
adds a CRC32C checksum, and
.B \-f
a file system capacity report.
.TP
.BR grep ", " egrep ", " fgrep
Print lines matching basic regular expressions, or extended ones with
.B \-E
(egrep), or fixed strings with
.B \-F
(fgrep); the two conflict with status 2.
.B \-e
adds a pattern and
.B \-f
the lines of a file, and with either every operand is a file; a newline
separates patterns.
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-h ,
and
.B \-r
ignore case, invert, match whole lines, number lines, omit file prefixes, and
recurse, and
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
keeps
.B \-r
on one file system.
.B \-c
counts,
.B \-l
lists matching files and overrides
.BR \-c ,
.B \-q
exits 0 at the first match, and
.B \-s
hides unreadable-file messages. In a file with a NUL byte, matches before the
line of the first NUL print, the next one shows a single warning, and later ones
print nothing, unless
.BR \-a ", " \-\-text
treats it as text. In a UTF-8 locale a dot and a bracket expression match one
character, and one byte otherwise. The status is 0 when a line is selected, 1
when none is, and 2 on error.
.IP
.BR \-\-color [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
takes auto (the default), always, or never. GREP_COLORS holds colon-separated
GNU capabilities such as ms=01;31: ms, mt, sl, cx, fn, ln, se, and rv apply,
unknown ones are ignored, and an empty value turns that color off. GREP_COLOR
sets the match color below GREP_COLORS.
.TP
.BR head ", " tail
Print the first or last ten lines.
.B \-n
sets a line count and
.B \-c
a byte count, with an optional suffix: b for 512, k, K, m, M, G, T, P, E, Z, Y,
R, or Q for powers of 1024, and those letters followed by B for powers of 1000
or by iB, as in 1kB and 1KiB. A first argument such as \-5 means
.BR "\-n 5" .
For head, a leading dash prints all but the last N; for tail, a leading plus
starts at line or byte N. Headers appear for several files;
.BR \-q ,
.BR \-\-quiet ,
and
.B \-\-silent
suppress them and
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
forces them, the last winning.
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero\-terminated
uses NUL line ends.
.IP
.B "tail \-f"
keeps printing what is appended to each regular file, and
.B \-F
follows by name, reopening a replaced, removed, or new file, and implies
.BR \-\-retry .
.BI \-\-follow= descriptor|name
selects the mode,
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sleep\-interval
the longest wait between checks (1 second), and
.BI \-\-pid= pid
ends following after that process exits. An interrupted follow exits with
status 130.
.TP
.B id
Print the user and group identities of the current process or of a user.
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user ,
.BR \-g ", " \-\-group ,
and
.BR \-G ", " \-\-groups
print only the user, the group, or every group,
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name
prints them in text form, and
.BR \-r ", " \-\-real
uses the real identities. \-n alone, or more than one of \-u, \-g, and \-G,
returns 2. An unknown user returns 1.
.TP
.BR killall ", " pkill
.B killall
signals processes by exact name, and
.B pkill
those whose short command contains the pattern. The signal comes from
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signal
in killall, and from
.B \-\-signal
or
.BI \- SIGNAL
in pkill;
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
lists signals. pkill
.BR \-s ", " \-\-session
takes comma-separated session ids, 0 for its own, and then the pattern is
optional; Windows has no sessions, so 0 is rejected and nothing matches another
id.
.B \-\-inverse
signals what the pattern and sessions do not select. Process 0, the shell, and
the utility itself are never signaled by killall.
.TP
.B less
Show files or standard input full screen on the alternate screen. The keys
come from the terminal. Space, f, Ctrl\-F, Ctrl\-V, and Page Down move forward a
page, b, Ctrl\-B, and Page Up back a page, d and Ctrl\-D forward half a page, u
and Ctrl\-U back half a page, j, e, Enter, Down, Ctrl\-E, and Ctrl\-N one row
down, and k, y, Up, Ctrl\-P, and Ctrl\-Y one row up. The mouse wheel scrolls
three rows. g, <, and Home go to the top, G, >, and End to the bottom, and r and
Ctrl\-L redraw.
.BI / pattern
and
.BI ? pattern
search forward and backward with an extended regular expression, and n and N
repeat the search. An empty pattern repeats the previous one, Escape cancels
the prompt, and an invalid pattern is reported. q and Q quit, and an interrupt
quits with status 130.
.IP
The status row shows the file, the visible lines and their total, the
position, and (END) at the end. Input loads as needed and is kept in memory up
to 128 MiB, counting the line records. Input beyond that is not shown, and the
status row adds (truncated) at the end.
.BR \-N ", " \-\-LINE\-NUMBERS
numbers the lines,
.BR \-S ", " \-\-chop\-long\-lines
cuts long lines instead of wrapping them, and
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
ignores case in searches. Several files follow headers of colons. When output
is not a terminal, the input is copied unchanged.
.TP
.B link
Create a hard link file2 to file1. An existing file2 returns 1.
.TP
.B ln
Create hard links, or symbolic links with
.BR \-s .
.B \-f
removes an existing destination,
.B \-L
follows a link source, and
.B \-P
links the link itself.
.TP
.B locale
Print LANG and the LC_ variables as the environment sets them.
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all\-locales
lists the available locales and
.BR \-m ", " \-\-charmaps
the character maps. A keyword operand such as charmap prints its value,
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keyword\-name
adds the keyword, and
.BR \-c ", " \-\-category\-name
adds its category. An unknown keyword, or a category operand such as
LC_CTYPE, returns 1.
.TP
.B logger
Send a message to the system log. The message is the operands, the whole file
of
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file ,
or standard input when neither is given, and \-f with operands returns 2. The
default tag is logger and the default priority user.notice.
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tag
sets the tag,
.BR \-p ", " \-\-priority
the facility and severity, as in user.notice,
.BR \-i ", " \-\-id
adds the process id, and
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stderr
also writes the message to standard error. An invalid priority returns 1.
.TP
.B logname
Print the login user, which can differ from the effective user that
.B whoami
prints.
.TP
.B ls
List sorted entries with
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-h .
.B \-F
appends type indicators,
.B \-p
a slash to directories,
.B \-d
lists directories themselves,
.B \-n
shows numeric ids, and
.B \-g
and
.B \-o
omit the owner or group.
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-S ,
and
.B \-r
sort by time, by size, and in reverse.
.B \-R
recurses,
.B \-\-tree
prints a tree,
.B \-L
limits depth, and
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
keeps both on one file system.
.B \-k
is accepted. Without a terminal, ls prints one entry per line, and
.B \-l
prints a symbolic link without its target.
.TP
.B make
Run make rules.
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file ,
.BR \-C ", " \-\-directory ,
.B \-B
(always rebuild),
.B \-k
(keep going), and the POSIX modes
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-q ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.B \-t
are supported, and
.B \-j
takes an optional job count passed through MAKEFLAGS. Without
.BR \-f ,
make reads Makefile or makefile, and
.B \-f \-
reads standard input. Operands of the form
.IR name = value
set variables, and the variables of the environment are also available.
.PP
.RS
The functions are abspath, addprefix, addsuffix, and, basename, call, dir,
error, eval, filter, filter-out, findstring, firstword, flavor, foreach, if,
join, lastword, notdir, or, origin, patsubst, realpath, shell, sort, strip,
subst, suffix, value, warning, wildcard, word, wordlist, and words. The
directives are include, ifeq, ifneq, ifdef, ifndef, else, endif, define,
override, export, and unexport, and the special targets are .DEFAULT, .IGNORE,
\&.PHONY, .POSIX, .PRECIOUS, .SCCS_GET, .SILENT, and .SUFFIXES.
.RE
.IP
The wildcard function and a prerequisite holding *, ?, or [ use shell pathname
expansion, and an unmatched prerequisite pattern stays as written. Recipes are
printed before they run and use the SHELL of the makefile, and MAKE expands to
koshkit make. A target without a rule returns 2, and
.B \-q
returns 1 when a target is out of date.
.TP
.B man
Print the source of the manual page of each name, from the section operand or
the first section that has one, searching the directories of MANPATH, by
default /usr/share/man and /usr/local/share/man.
Only uncompressed pages in man\fIN\fR/\fIname\fR.\fIN\fR are found.
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keyword
lists the pages whose name or source contains the keyword, ignoring ASCII
case, returns 1 when none does, and returns 2 with operands. A missing page
returns 1.
.TP
.B meow
Show a screensaver of pixel-art cats.
.BR \-f ", " \-\-fps
sets frames per second (1 to 240, default 60),
.BR \-s ", " \-\-speed
the speed (0.1 to 10), and
.BR \-n ", " \-\-cats
the number of cats (1 to 8). Space pauses, + and \- change speed, and any other
key exits.
.TP
.B mkdir
Create directories, with parents under
.BR \-p .
.B \-m
takes an octal mode or a symbolic mode applied to 777.
.TP
.B mkfifo
Create each FIFO, with the mode of
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mode
or 0666 less the creation mask.
.TP
.B mknod
Create FIFOs and character or block special files from POSIX type operands or
.BR \-\-type ,
.BR \-\-fifo ,
.BR \-\-character ,
and
.BR \-\-block .
Device nodes take
.B \-\-major
and
.BR \-\-minor ,
and
.B \-m
sets the mode.
.TP
.B more
Show files or standard input a screen at a time with a
.B \-\-More\-\-
prompt, with keys from the terminal: space and f the next page, Enter, j, and
Down the next line, d half a page, b, k, Page Up, and Up further back, g and G
the top and the bottom, the searches of
.BR less ,
and q, or a forward key at the end, to quit.
.BR \-n ", " \-\-lines
sets the lines per screen,
.BR \-c ", " \-\-clear
clears before each page,
.BR \-e ", " \-\-exit
exits after the last line,
.BR \-p ", " \-\-command
starts at the first line that matches a pattern,
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tag
at a tag of the tags file,
.BR \-s ", " \-\-squeeze
collapses repeated blank lines, and
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
ignores case in searches. Each of several files follows a header of colons,
also when output is not a terminal. When output is not a terminal, the input
is copied, changed only by
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-t ,
and
.BR \-s .
A pattern of
.B \-p
may begin with a slash.
.B \-t
reads ./tags, whose lines hold a tag, a file, and a line separated by tabs, and
takes no file operand.
.B \-p
and
.B \-t
cannot be combined, and more needs a file when standard input is a terminal.
The status row shows \-\-More\-\- and the position, or (END).
Input is kept in memory up to 128 MiB, counting the line records. Input
beyond that limit is not shown, and the status row adds (truncated).
.B \-u
is accepted.
.TP
.B mv
Rename with
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B nice
Run a utility with its scheduling priority lowered by 10, or by the increment
of
.BR \-n ", " \-\-increment .
A utility that is not found returns 127, and one that cannot run 126.
.TP
.B nl
Number lines within logical pages. A line of only the section delimiter
repeated three, two, or one times starts a header, body, or footer and prints
empty.
.B \-d
sets the delimiter, \e: by default, adding a colon to a single character; an
empty delimiter disables sections.
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-b ,
and
.B \-f
set the section styles (default n, t, n): a every line, t nonempty lines, n
none, and p\fIBRE\fR lines matching the regular expression. Each section
restarts at
.B \-v
unless
.B \-p
is given, and
.B \-i
sets the increment; both may be negative.
.B \-l
numbers every Nth empty line of a run under style a.
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.B \-s
set the width (at most 65536), the ln, rn, or rz format, and the separator. An
overflowing line number stops with status 1.
.TP
.B nohup
Run a utility that ignores SIGHUP. When standard output is a terminal, the
output is appended to nohup.out in the current directory, or in HOME when that
fails, which is created with mode 0600. A terminal standard input is replaced
with the null device, and a terminal standard error follows standard output. A
utility that is not found, or a nohup.out that cannot be opened, returns 127,
and a utility that cannot run returns 126.
.TP
.B nproc
Print the available processor count;
.B \-\-all
and
.BI \-\-ignore= COUNT
are supported.
.TP
.B od
Dump bytes in the formats of
.BR \-t ;
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.B \-x
select o1, c, u2, o2, d2, and x2. A line holds 16 bytes, or
.BR \-w [ \fIN\fR ]
or
.BR \-\-width [= \fIN\fR ]
bytes, up to 65536 or 32 without N. A width that is not a multiple of the
largest unit is replaced by that unit with a warning. The types of
.B \-t
are c, o, u, d, and x, with an optional size, and several types print one row
each.
.B \-A
sets the address radix to d, o, x, or n for none,
.B \-j
skips bytes,
.B \-N
limits the bytes read, and
.B \-v
prints repeated lines that are otherwise shown as *.
.TP
.B paste
Join corresponding lines with tabs.
.B \-d
sets delimiters used in turn,
.B \-s
joins each file into one line, and
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero\-terminated
uses NUL line ends.
.TP
.B pathchk
Check paths against the file system limits.
.B \-p
uses the POSIX portable limits and characters,
.B \-P
rejects an empty path and a component starting with a hyphen, and
.BR \-W ", " \-\-windows
rejects what Windows cannot hold, as Windows builds always do.
.TP
.B pr
Paginate each file or standard input into 66-line pages with a header of the
date, the file, and the page number. +\fIfirst\fR[:\fIlast\fR] selects pages,
.RI \- columns
prints that many columns,
.BR \-a ", " \-\-across
fills them across,
.BR \-m ", " \-\-merge
prints the files side by side, and
.BR \-t ", " \-\-omit\-header
omits headers.
.BR \-d ", " \-\-double\-space ,
.BR \-n ", " \-\-number\-lines ,
.BR \-o ", " \-\-indent ,
.BR \-h ", " \-\-header ,
.BR \-D ", " \-\-date\-format ,
.BR \-l ", " \-\-length ,
.BR \-w ", " \-\-width ,
and
.BR \-s ", " \-\-separator
set spacing, numbering, the indent, the header text and date format, the page
length and width, and the column separator.
.BR \-F " and " \-f
separate pages with form feeds, and
.BR \-r ", " \-\-no\-file\-warnings
suppresses file warnings.
.TP
.B printenv
Print the environment, or the given variables, with status 1 if one is
absent.
.TP
.B ps
List processes with
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-u ,
and
.BR \-x ;
.B \-w
is accepted.
.TP
.B readlink
Print the target of each symbolic link. A non-link is reported with status 1.
.BR \-f ", " \-\-canonicalize ,
.BR \-e ", " \-\-canonicalize\-existing ,
and
.BR \-m ", " \-\-canonicalize\-missing
print the canonical absolute path with every link resolved, as
.B realpath
prints it without a flag, with
.BR \-e ,
and with
.BR \-m .
The last of the three wins.
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-newline
omits the newline for a single file.
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.B \-\-silent
suppress the error messages.
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
ends each entry with a NUL byte.
.TP
.B realpath
Print the canonical absolute path. Every component but the last must exist,
all with
.BR \-e ,
and none with
.BR \-m .
Links resolve as met, or with
.BR \-P ", " \-\-physical ;
.BR \-L ", " \-\-logical
resolves .. first, and
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-no\-symlinks ,
and
.B \-\-strip
leave links alone, the last of the three winning.
.B \-\-relative\-to
prints paths relative to a directory, and
.B \-\-relative\-base
prints paths below a directory relative to it and others absolute; with both,
paths are relative to the first when it is below the base.
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
omits errors, and
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
ends entries with NUL.
.TP
.B renice
Add the increment of the required
.BR \-n ", " \-\-increment
to the priority of each process, or of each process group with
.BR \-g ", " \-\-pgrp ,
or of each user's processes with
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user .
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid
selects process ids, the default.
.TP
.B retry
Repeat a command until it succeeds or the attempts run out.
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-m ,
and
.B \-q
set the attempt count, initial delay, backoff factor, maximum delay, and quiet
mode.
.TP
.B rm
Remove paths.
.BR \-r ", " \-R ", " \-\-recursive
removes directories and their contents,
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dir
removes empty directories,
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
ignores missing paths and never asks,
.B \-i
asks before each removal,
.B \-I
asks once before removing more than three paths or a tree, and
.BI \-\-interactive= when
selects never, once, or always.
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
prints each removed path, and
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints what would be removed and removes nothing. The operands . and .. are
refused, and so is every spelling of a root directory or a Windows drive root
unless
.B \-\-no\-preserve\-root
is given.
.B \-\-preserve\-root=all
also refuses an operand on another device than its parent.
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
skips directories on other file systems with status 1. A missing path returns
1 without
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B rmdir
Remove empty directories, and with
.B \-p
their empty parents.
.TP
.B sed
Apply an editing script, from the first operand or from each
.BR \-e ", " \-\-expression
and
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file ,
to the lines of each file or standard input. The commands are s with the g and
p flags, y, d, p, q, =, and a, i, and c, whose text follows on the same line or
on the next lines after a backslash, with line, $, and regex addresses and
ranges, and ! after an address negates it. The commands n, N, l, r, and w, the
numeric, w, and I substitution flags, and the addr,+N and 0,/re/ addresses are
rejected with status 1.
.BR \-n ", " \-\-quiet
suppresses automatic printing, and
.BR \-E ", " \-\-extended\-regexp
selects extended regular expressions. Blocks, the hold space, branches, and
in-place editing are not supported, and an unsupported command returns 1.
.TP
.B seq
Print numbers with a nonzero step. Integers print exactly, and decimal,
exponent, and hexadecimal operands print with the most fraction digits of the
first operand and the step.
.BR \-w ", " \-\-equal\-width
pads with zeros,
.BR \-s ", " \-\-separator
sets the separator, and
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format
applies one printf %e, %f, %g, or %a conversion and excludes
.BR \-w .
.TP
.B sleep
Pause for the sum of the durations, with optional s, m, h, or d suffixes.
.TP
.BR slurp ", " vi ", " emacs
Edit text files as the
.B slurp
builtin does, with the same options and +\fILINE\fR operand. As
.BR vi
it runs with
.BR \-V ,
and as
.B emacs
with
.BR \-e .
.TP
.B sort
Sort lines by byte order or by
.B \-k
keys of the form
.IR F[.C][bdfghiMnrV][,F[.C][bdfghiMnrV]] .
.B \-t
sets the separator.
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.B \-r
ignore leading blanks, compare only blanks and alphanumerics, fold case,
compare only printable characters, compare numbers, and reverse.
.B \-g
compares floating numbers, including exponents, infinities, and NaN, with text
first;
.B \-h
compares K, M, G, T, P, E, Z, Y, R, and Q suffixes;
.B \-M
compares month abbreviations with other text first; and
.B \-V
compares version numbers. At most one of
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-M ,
and
.B \-n
applies to a key, and none combines with
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-i ,
or
.BR \-V .
.B \-z
uses NUL line ends,
.B \-u
keeps the first of equal keys,
.B \-c
reports the first unsorted line and
.B \-C
only the status,
.B \-o
writes to a file,
.B \-s
skips the last-resort whole-line comparison, and
.B \-m
is accepted.
.TP
.B split
Split a file or standard input into pieces of 1000 lines, the count of
.BR \-l ", " \-\-lines ,
or the byte count of
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bytes ,
called xaa, xab, and so on, or with the prefix operand.
.BR \-a ", " \-\-suffix\-length
sets the number of suffix letters. The byte count is a plain decimal number. A
count of 0, a count with a suffix such as 1k, and running out of suffixes
return 1.
.TP
.B stat
Print file or file system metadata.
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-c ,
and
.B \-\-printf
follow links, select the file system, select terse output, apply a format,
and apply a format without a trailing newline.
.TP
.B strings
Print each run of at least four printable characters in each file or standard
input.
.BR \-n ", " \-\-bytes
sets the minimum length,
.BR \-t ", " \-\-radix
prefixes each string with its offset in d, o, or x. The whole file is always
scanned, so
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
has no effect.
.TP
.B stty
Report or change terminal settings;
.B \-a
reports all, and
.B \-g
prints a state that restores them later.
.TP
.B sync
Flush file system data.
.B \-d
flushes the data of each operand, and
.B \-f
the file system of each operand, or every file system without one.
.TP
.B tabs
Set the terminal tab stops to every 8 columns, every N columns for \-N, the
POSIX template of \-a, \-a2, \-c, \-c2, \-c3, \-f, \-p, \-s, or \-u, or the
comma-separated columns of one operand, by writing escape sequences to standard
output. A column with + is relative to the previous stop, and the stops must
increase within column 160. A second operand and \-T return 1.
.TP
.B tee
Copy standard input to files, appending with
.BR \-a .
.TP
.B timeout
Run a command with a time limit, with an optional duration suffix.
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signal
selects the signal,
.BR \-k ", " \-\-kill\-after
sends KILL after a further duration, and
.BR \-p ", " \-\-preserve\-status
returns the command status.
.TP
.B touch
Update file times.
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-r ,
and
.B \-t
select the access time, skip missing files, select the modification time, copy
a reference file, and set a timestamp.
.TP
.B tput
Write the escape sequence of a terminal capability from a fixed table. The
capabilities are bel, blink, bold, civis, clear, cnorm, cub1, cud1, cuf1,
cuu1, dch1, dl1, ed, el, home, ich1, il1, ind, init, reset, rev, rmso, rmul,
sgr0, smso, smul, and cup with a row and a column. cols and lines print the
terminal size and need a terminal, and longname prints the value of TERM, or of
.BR \-T ", " \-\-terminal ,
followed by the word terminal. The sequences do not depend on TERM. An unknown
capability returns 1.
.TP
.B tr
Translate bytes with ranges, classes, [=c=] equivalence classes, and [c*n]
repeats.
.B \-d
deletes,
.B \-c
and
.B \-C
complement the first set, and
.B \-s
squeezes runs of a byte from the last set. A shorter second set is padded with
its last byte unless
.BR \-t ", " \-\-truncate\-set1
truncates the first. When translating, the second set accepts only [:upper:]
and [:lower:], each aligned with an [:upper:] or [:lower:] class in the first
set, and one [c*] repeat that fills it to the length of the first; a [c*] in
the first set is an error.
.TP
.B tsort
Print a total order of the pairs of words in a file or standard input. A cycle
prints a partial order and returns 1, and an odd number of words returns 1.
.TP
.B tty
Print the terminal of standard input, or not a tty with status 1.
.BR \-s ", " \-\-silent
prints nothing.
.TP
.B uname
Print the system name, or the fields of
.BR \-m " (machine), " \-n " (node), " \-r " (release), " \-s " (system), and " \-v
(version), or all of them with
.BR \-a .
.TP
.B uniq
Collapse adjacent equal lines.
.B \-c
counts,
.B \-d
and
.B \-u
print only repeated or only unique lines, and
.B \-D
every line of a repeated run.
.B \-f
and
.B \-s
skip fields and characters,
.B \-w
compares at most N characters, and
.B \-i
ignores ASCII case.
.BR \-\-all\-repeated [= \fInone\fR | \fIprepend\fR | \fIseparate\fR ]
acts as
.B \-D
with empty lines before or between runs, and
.BR \-\-group [= \fIseparate\fR | \fIprepend\fR | \fIappend\fR | \fIboth\fR ]
prints every line with empty lines between, before, or after runs and excludes
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-D ,
and
.BR \-u .
.B \-z
uses NUL line ends, and a second operand is the output file.
.TP
.B unlink
Remove one non-directory path.
.TP
.B watch
Run a command at an interval.
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-g ,
and
.B \-e
set the interval, omit the title, exit when output changes, and exit after a
command error;
.B \-x
is accepted. On a terminal, q or an interrupt exits with status 0, and the
command reads cooked terminal input.
.TP
.B wc
Count lines, words, characters, and bytes with
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-m ,
and
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B which
Resolve builtins, PATH programs, and bundled utilities, with
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
and
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet .
.TP
.B who
Print the logged-in users with their terminals and login times. Only user
records are read, so \-b, \-d, \-l, \-p, \-r, and \-t print nothing, and \-a
prints the users.
.BR \-H " adds headings, " \-m " and " "am i"
select the terminal of standard input, and
.B \-q
prints the users and their count.
.B \-T
prints ? for the message status,
.B \-u
prints . for the idle time, and
.B \-s
has no effect.
.TP
.B whoami
Print the effective user.
.TP
.B xargs
Append arguments from standard input to a command, which reads the null device.
.BR \-a ", " \-\-arg\-file
reads arguments from a file and leaves the command standard input.
.B \-0
splits on NUL and
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delimiter
on one character or escape such as \en, \et, \ex41, or \e0; both keep quotes and
backslashes literal and disable the end marker.
.B \-r
skips an empty input,
.B \-E
sets the end marker, and
.BR \-e [ \fIEOF\fR "], " \-\-eof [= \fIEOF\fR ]
sets or removes it.
.B \-I
replaces a string with each input line, and
.BR \-i [ \fIR\fR "], " \-\-replace [= \fIR\fR ]
does so with R or {}.
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.B \-s
limit each command to lines, arguments, or bytes; for
.BR \-L ,
empty lines do not count and a trailing blank continues a line.
.BR \-P ", " \-\-max\-procs
runs that many commands at once, or as many as possible for 0, with output that
may interleave.
.B \-p
asks on the terminal before each command,
.B \-t
traces, and
.B \-x
fails when a command does not fit. The status is 123 when a command fails, 124
when one exits with 255, 125 when a signal ends one, and 126 or 127 when one
cannot run, which under
.B \-P
stops the run after the running commands finish.
.TP
.B yes
Repeat the joined operands, or y, until output closes.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A diagnostic is written to standard error. It begins with the file, line, and
column, then
.BR error ,
.BR warning ,
or
.BR note ,
and the message, followed by the source line and a caret under the column. A
finding of analysis ends with its check code in parentheses, such as (SC2086),
and a
.B note:
line may suggest a fix. Text built at run time is its own source, so a
location in an eval string appears as
.BR eval:2:7: ,
in a trap action as
.BR trap:1:5: ,
and in a
.B \-c
string as
.BR \-c:1:5: .
Standard input and interactive lines have no label. Each function call,
sourced file, eval, trap action, subshell, and command, function, or process
substitution between the top level and the failing command adds a backtrace
frame beginning with
.BR trace: ,
and a repeated frame is printed once with a repeat count. With several
.B \-c
strings or files, the frame pointing into the command line is printed once
after the diagnostics of that string or file. Diagnostics read the same in
every mood. On a color terminal the labels and codes are colored.
.PP
Linting, the language server, and script diagnostics report every syntax
error in one run. A construct unavailable in the selected mood, such as
.B [[
in the
.B sh
mood, is reported, and its contents are checked for further syntax errors.
.PP
Analysis checks a script before it runs. It has strict, lenient, and annoying
tiers, which
.B \-W
selects as described under
.BR OPTIONS ,
and
.B \-\-list\-diagnostics
lists every check. In the
.B kosh
mood analysis runs by default, and strict and lenient findings reject the
script, which then exits with status 1 without running. They include the
expansion of a variable that is never assigned, a call without arguments to a
function that uses them, and a call to a function before its definition. An
assignment in a function to a variable not declared
.B local
is reported. In the
.B sh
mood, which has no
.BR local ,
only a variable first set in a function and also used outside it is reported.
In the other moods analysis runs only at a level that
.B \-W
or
.B set \-W
selects, and its findings are warnings. An interactive shell analyzes each line
before it runs it. A script operand without a shebang is reported when the
level is not 0.
.PP
Analysis follows static source operands into readable files. It checks a
command, backtick, or process substitution as a subshell, and a function
substitution in the current shell. When more than one finding is reported, a
summary such as
.B Encountered 1 warning and 2 errors.
follows them.
.PP
A comment such as
.B # shellcheck disable=SC2086,unquoted-command-substitution
suppresses analysis checks. A numeric code suppresses every variant under it,
a slug only its own variant, and the value may be
.B all
or a range such as
.BR SC2000\-SC2100 .
Before the first command it covers the file, and later it covers the next
and-or command. It does not suppress parser or runtime diagnostics.
.PP
An unavailable command or path is reported through its first unavailable
component when that can be identified. An internal error stops the shell with
an
.B error:
line and
.B note:
lines identifying the process and build, and the shell aborts.
.SH LINTING
.B kosh \-\-lint
analyzes shell source without running it, with every diagnostic tier enabled
at the severity of the mood, regardless of
.BR \-W ,
.BR \-\-no\-diagnostics ,
and
.BR \-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics .
Each command string is analyzed in order, then each file operand, or standard
input when neither is given. Startup and settings files are skipped. The
diagnostics go to standard error, and standard output stays empty.
.PP
Shell commands are also extracted from CI configurations, Ansible, Dockerfile,
Compose, Markdown, Makefile, justfile, Kubernetes, and similar hosts, and only
the extracted sh, bash, or kosh source is checked. A file counts as shell
source by its extension or shebang, and a file in no recognized format fails
with status 1 and a list of the formats. A host interpreter setting, such as a
step shell, a SHELL assignment or instruction, a shebang, or an executable
option, selects the shell, and a command for another interpreter is skipped. An
exec array contributes only the script of a shell
.B \-c
command. A justfile recipe line, joined across backslash continuations, is one
command, and a shebang or script recipe is one script. A Jinja template of a
shell script, such as
.IR name .sh.j2,
is checked with its template syntax masked. Host template expressions and
editor variables are masked in every format, interpolated values count as
plain words, and an unresolved command is not reported in a fragment that holds
an interpolation. Variables that the host provides, such as CI variables,
exported recipe parameters, and Dockerfile ARG and ENV values, count as set.
Diagnostics point into the host file.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-apply ,
safe structured fixes are written to each file, a summary of the fixes is
printed, and only the diagnostics that remain are reported. A fixed file that
would no longer parse is left unchanged. A warning-only result exits with
status 0, and a remaining error or an input or write failure exits with status
1.
.SH FORMATTING
.B kosh \-\-format
formats shell source without running it. Structural keywords and compound
braces go on separate lines, each nested level indents by two spaces, direct
.B test
commands become bracket commands unless an earlier alias or function shadows
them, code wraps at 80 columns at a safe token boundary, and lines end in LF.
With no file or a lone
.BR \- ,
standard input is formatted, and otherwise one file is formatted to standard
output, highlighted when it is a terminal. In a host format, only the shell
fragments that the host allows to change are formatted, such as Markdown code
blocks. A syntax error is reported and exits with status 1. Combined with
.BR \-\-lint ,
the lint fixes are applied first.
.PP
.B \-\-apply
replaces each file operand with the result. A changed file is replaced
atomically through a temporary file beside it, and its mode and symbolic links
are kept. A file that is not a regular file, or one that changed while it was
processed, is refused.
.SH LANGUAGE SERVER
.B kosh \-\-as\-language\-server
runs a language server over standard input and output for editor integration.
It reports diagnostics after each document change and provides completion,
quick fixes, a fix-all action for non-conflicting safe fixes, formatting,
definition lookup, hover information, semantic highlighting, a document
outline, and rename. Completion is triggered by a space or a dash.
.PP
The server works inside the shell regions of the host formats that
.B \-\-lint
recognizes and follows relative sources from the document directory. Rename
updates variables, functions, and aliases in the open document and keeps the
sigil and braces of
.B $name
and
.BR ${name} .
A command can be renamed only when the document defines it as a function or
alias. The mood of a document comes from a recognized shebang, then from a
language identifier of kosh, bash, sh, or bash-posix, with rbash and
shellscript meaning bash, then from the extension .kosh, .bash, .sh, or .dash,
and otherwise it is
.BR kosh .
The server skips startup and settings files, and it exits with status 0 after
a shutdown request and 1 otherwise.
.SH LOCALE
The first nonempty value of LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE, and LANG selects the character
locale. A UTF-8 or utf8 codeset selects UTF-8 when the platform supports it,
and anything else selects bytes. In UTF-8, a ? and each step of a * consume one
character, a bracket expression compares code points, ${#v} and
${v:offset:length} count characters, and ${v^}, ${v,}, and ${v~} change the
case of multibyte letters. This applies to pathname expansion, case, [[ == ]],
${v#pat}, ${v%pat}, ${v/pat/rep}, ${v^pat}, and compgen \-X. Completion always
matches by UTF-8 sequence.
.PP
In the Bash moods pathname expansion sorts by the collation order that LC_ALL
or LC_COLLATE selects. The collation order is read from the environment the
first time a pattern is sorted. The first nonempty value of LC_ALL, LC_NUMERIC,
and LANG selects the numeric locale of
.BR printf .
.SH WINDOWS
On Windows the shell runs the same grammar. A path of /dev/null opens the null
device, and /tmp or a path under it maps into the directory in TEMP. The
options compatibility.dev_null and compatibility.slash_tmp, both on, govern the
two mappings. Variable names are not case-sensitive, PATH uses semicolons, and
the user settings directory falls back to USERPROFILE when HOME is unset. The
Bash startup files keep their POSIX paths. A subshell runs in a new process.
Process substitution, echo, pwd \-W, and the local form of assimilate behave as
their entries describe.
.SH RESTRICTED SHELL
A restricted shell rejects directory changes, including those of pushd, popd,
and z, and changes to SHELL, PATH, ENV, and BASH_ENV, which become read-only.
It rejects a directory separator in a command word or a source operand, the
redirections >, >|, <>, >>, &>, &>>, and >&,
.BR exec ,
.BR newgrp ,
.BR bench ,
.BR assimilate ,
and
.BR slurp ,
.BR "hash \-p" ,
.BR "command \-p" ,
.BR "enable \-f" ,
.BR "enable \-d" ,
.BR "set \-M" ,
.BR "set \-I" ,
.BR "set +I" ,
.BR "set +r" ,
.BR "source \-\-mood" ,
history file operands, every form of
.BR koshkit ,
and the
.B koshconf
forms
.BR create ,
.BR set ,
.BR load ,
and
.BR "save \-\-persist" .
Numeric descriptor duplication stays available, and so does
.BR "set \-o posix" .
.PP
A command word falls back only to a bundled utility that a startup file enabled
with
.BR enable .
After startup,
.B enable
can disable a builtin but cannot enable one or change a bundled utility. An
enabled utility that starts other programs, such as
.BR env ,
.BR xargs ,
.BR find ,
.BR nohup ,
.BR timeout ,
or
.BR watch ,
can start any program, including another shell, so a startup file for a
restricted shell should enable only utilities that start none.
.PP
No setting turns restricted mode off. A shell started restricted by
.B \-r
or as rbash ignores and removes
.B KOSHCONF
with a warning, and applies only presentation and diagnostic options from the
settings files. It ignores the mood, a semantic option,
editor.history.file_path, and editor.history.max_entries there with a warning.
A child shell inherits the restrictions, and a sourced script keeps them. A
shell script found through PATH and run as a command runs without them.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /etc/kosh.conf ", " %ProgramData%\ekosh\ekosh.conf
The system settings file on POSIX and on Windows, described in
.BR kosh (5).
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf ", " ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf
The user settings file.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh ", " ~/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh
The rc script of an interactive or login
.B kosh
mood shell.
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_history
The default history file, as described under
.BR HISTORY .
The shell writes temporary files beside it while it rewrites it.
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_directory_history
The default directory history of
.BR z .
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_calc_history
The default history of
.BR calc .
.TP
.IR /etc/profile ", " ~/.profile ", " ~/.bash_profile ", " ~/.bash_login ", " ~/.bashrc ", " /etc/bash.bashrc
The startup files of the
.B sh
and Bash moods, as described under
.BR "STARTUP FILES" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kosh (5),
